\pard\plain\keepn\sb400\sa140\f2\b\fs34\cf2 Conventions\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Code is set in a monospaced face and is real, compilable tsbRapidFX unless the surrounding text says otherwise. Shell commands are shown the same way; a leading {\f1\fs18 $} is never part of what you type.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Diagnostic codes look like {\f1\fs18 RFX0208}. The code is the contract: the wording of a message may change between versions, the code does not. Every code has an entry in the compiler's own message reference.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Names of files, keywords, types and methods appear as {\f1\fs18 Files.readString}. Keyword casing in running text follows the manual's own habit \u8212? {\f1\fs18 SessionStatic}, {\f1\fs18 Dim} \u8212? but the compiler does not care: keywords are case-insensitive, names are not. That distinction is Chapter 3.\par
\pard\plain\page\keepn\sb0\sa280\qc\f2\b\fs52\cf2 Part I \u8212? The compiler and the language\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb400\sa140\f2\b\fs34\cf2 1 What tsbRapidFX is\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj tsbRapidFX is a programming language with a Basic grammar that compiles {\b directly to JVM class files}. It is not a transpiler: no Java source is written on the way, and there is no runtime layer between your code and the platform.\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Hello.rfx\line 
  -> Lexer      line oriented; '_' and an open bracket continue a line\line 
  -> Parser     recursive descent, Pratt for expressions   -> syntax tree\line 
  -> Binder     names -> symbols, expressions -> types     -> bound tree\line 
       :        resolved against the real class path\line 
  -> Lowerer    If / For / While / Do  ->  labels and jumps\line 
  -> Emitter    ASM  ->  .class\line 
  + the tsbRapidFX runtime jar\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The three ideas behind it\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Java is used, not rebuilt.} A tsbRapidFX {\f1\fs18 Integer} {\i is} an {\f1\fs18 int}. A tsbRapidFX {\f1\fs18 String} {\i is} a {\f1\fs18 java.lang.String}. A tsbRapidFX {\f1\fs18 Class} {\i is} a Java class, and a {\f1\fs18 Module} is a Java class with static methods. There is no marshalling at the boundary because there is no boundary. That is why the whole JDK is open to you, why a Java call costs nothing, and why Java can call back into tsbRapidFX without an adapter.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj What {\i is} rebuilt is only what Java does not have: the Basic intrinsics \u8212? {\f1\fs18 Left$}, {\f1\fs18 Format$}, {\f1\fs18 MsgBox}, {\f1\fs18 Now} \u8212? as a runtime library of about eighty functions. That is a finite set. Copying out the JDK would not be.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Statically typed.} Every value has a type that is fixed at compile time. Widening conversions happen by themselves ({\f1\fs18 Integer} to {\f1\fs18 Double}); narrowing ones you write ({\f1\fs18 CInt(x)}). This is the difference from VB6 and the reason tsbRapidFX needs neither boxing nor a runtime type check to do ordinary arithmetic.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Straight to bytecode means the debugger works.} The generated class carries {\f1\fs18 SourceFile: "Hello.rfx"} and a real {\f1\fs18 LineNumberTable}, so any Java debugger steps through the Basic source and a stack trace names the line you wrote:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 at Test.Check(Errors.rfx:48)\line 
at Test.Main(Errors.rfx:11)\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Local variable names are in the class file too, so a debugger shows them spelled the way you spelled them.\par
