\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b One symbol for both worlds.} {\f1\fs18 Symbol.Method} describes a tsbRapidFX {\f1\fs18 Sub} and a Java method with the same fields, so there is no separate call machinery for interop. The binder makes the same symbols out of a class file as out of a declaration, and from there {\f1\fs18 Show(1)} and {\f1\fs18 list.add("x")} run through identical code \u8212? overload resolution, argument conversion, emission.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b The class path is an input, not a setting.} {\f1\fs18 Compilation.create(source, path)}: {\f1\fs18 Dim b As Button} means a different type \u8212? or none \u8212? depending on what is on the path, and every message about a type is an answer relative to it. {\f1\fs18 rfxc -cp lib/flatlaf.jar} hands the same path to compiling {\i and} to running, so the two cannot drift apart.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Why the parser never gives up\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Completion always happens on broken code. {\f1\fs18 btn.} is syntactically incomplete, and that is exactly where the list should open. A compiler that stops at the first error can never serve an IDE, and changing that afterwards is not an extension but a rebuild of the front end. So:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b The parser always returns a complete tree.} A missing token is inserted zero-width; an unusable line becomes an error node and the next line parses normally. {\f1\fs18 btn.} therefore yields a member-access node whose target the binder can still type \u8212? and that node {\i is} the completion request.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Every node carries its exact source span.} {\f1\fs18 findAt(offset)} and {\f1\fs18 pathTo(offset)} answer "what is under the cursor". The containment test includes the end of the span, because the cursor after the dot sits exactly there.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b An unknown name reports itself once.} The error type is compatible with everything, so the operators it flows through stay quiet. A typo costs one message, not one per use.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Diagnostics are objects} with span, code and severity. There is no {\f1\fs18 System.err.println} anywhere in the compiler.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b No global mutable state.} A {\f1\fs18 Compilation} is immutable and cheap; an editor builds a new one on every keystroke and throws the old one away. Nothing to invalidate, nothing that can go stale.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj These promises have a test file of their own, kept apart from the language tests. A batch compiler would need none of them \u8212? it could stop at the first error and still be good. If one of them breaks, tsbRapidFX goes on compiling and only the IDE quietly stops working.\par
\pard\sb160\sa160\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb400\sa140\f2\b\fs34\cf2 2 The compiler: rfxc\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Building it\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 ./gradlew :cli:jar\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The result is {\f1\fs18 cli/build/libs/rfxc-0.1.0.jar} \u8212? one runnable jar holding the compiler and the runtime library.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The first program\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 hello.rfx}:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Sub Main()\line 
    Print "Hello, world!"\line 
End Sub\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 java -jar rfxc-0.1.0.jar --run hello.rfx\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Compiling only, which produces {\f1\fs18 hello.class}:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 java -jar rfxc-0.1.0.jar -o build hello.rfx\line 
java -cp build:rfxc-0.1.0.jar hello\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The second line shows two things at once. The result is an ordinary class file that {\f1\fs18 java} starts \u8212? and the runtime library has to be present, because {\f1\fs18 Print} is a call into it.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The invocation in detail\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 rfxc - the tsbRapidFX compiler\line 
\line 
Usage:\line 
  rfxc [options] <file.rfx> [more.rfx ...]\line 
  rfxc [options] <directory>\line 
  rfxc --project <x.rfxproj>\line 
\line 
Options:\line 
  -o, --out <directory>     where the .class files go (default: .)\line 
  -cp, --classpath <paths>  jars and directories to compile against,\line 
                            separated by ':' - FlatLaf, say\line 
      --project <x.rfxproj> build a whole project (its sources, libraries\line 
                            and jar name are named in the project file)\line 
      --jar <file.jar>      build a runnable jar instead of .class files\line 
                            (the runtime library goes inside it; -cp entries\line 
                            are named as Class-Path in the manifest)\line 
      --run                 start 'Sub Main' after compiling\line 
  -v, --verbose             list the files written\line 
      --no-color            no colour in error messages\line 
  -h, --help                this help\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 --classpath} applies to both compiling {\i and} {\f1\fs18 --run}. That is deliberate: compiling against one version of a library and running against another is the mistake that shows up later as a {\f1\fs18 NoSuchMethodError} inside a window.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Where Sub Main may live\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A file needs no {\f1\fs18 Module}. These two programs are equivalent:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Sub Main()\line 
    Print "Hello"\line 
End Sub\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Module Hello\line 
    Sub Main()\line 
        Print "Hello"\line 
    End Sub\line 
End Module\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Without a {\f1\fs18 Module} the class takes the name of the file. {\f1\fs18 hello.rfx} becomes {\f1\fs18 hello.class}, and {\f1\fs18 Sub Main} is findable in it both as {\f1\fs18 Main} and as {\f1\fs18 main(String[])} \u8212? Java is case-sensitive and tsbRapidFX is not, so both exist.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Reading an error message\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 hello.rfx:3:21: error RFX0300: Type 'Doesntexist' not found. Is an 'Imports' missing?\line 
  |\line 
3 |         Dim x As Doesntexist\line 
  |                  ^^^^^^^^^^^\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj File, line, column, a code, one sentence, and the place underneath. The code is stable: the wording may change, the code will not.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The compiler does not stop at the first error. It reports everything it finds, and a typo produces exactly one message \u8212? not one per use.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Passing a program on\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 --jar} builds a file that starts on its own:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 rfxc --jar greeting.jar Greeting.rfx\line 
java -jar greeting.jar\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b The tsbRapidFX runtime library is inside it.} Every program needs it \u8212? {\f1\fs18 Print} is a call into it, and so is {\f1\fs18 +} \u8212? so a jar without it would end at the first statement with a {\f1\fs18 NoClassDefFoundError}. It costs about thirty kilobytes and it is the entire point of the exercise: one file you can hand over.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Third-party libraries are not copied in.} Whatever arrived through {\f1\fs18 -cp} is named in the manifest as {\f1\fs18 Class-Path} instead:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 rfxc -cp lib/flatlaf-3.7.2.jar --jar program.jar Program.rfx\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The {\f1\fs18 lib} directory then has to sit beside the jar. Copying them in would mean redistributing somebody else's code under somebody else's licence, and thirty kilobytes would become a hundred megabytes.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Without a {\f1\fs18\b Sub Main} you get a library} \u8212? a jar with no {\f1\fs18 Main-Class}. That is not a mistake; it is how tsbRapidFX code is used from Java:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 System.out.println(Tools.Twice(21));   // 42\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Compiling twice gives the same file.} Every entry carries the same fixed timestamp, so two builds can be compared by checksum.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Projects\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A program may consist of any number of files. A type declared in one file is visible in all the others \u8212? there is nothing to import and nothing to register.\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 MyProgram/\line 
  MyProgram.rfxproj\line 
  src/\line 
    Main.rfx\line 
    MainWindow.rfx\line 
  lib/\line 
    flatlaf-3.7.2.jar\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The project file:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 ' This project can be built without an IDE:\line 
'     rfxc --project MyProgram.rfxproj\line 
\line 
Name    = MyProgram\line 
Sources = src\line 
Library = lib/flatlaf-3.7.2.jar\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
