\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Keys may repeat, order does not matter, and {\f1\fs18 '} starts a comment. The format cannot express more than that \u8212? and a format that cannot express more will not grow into a second language you have to learn.\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 {\b The keys are case-sensitive, and an unknown key is silently ignored} \u8212? later versions may add keys, so a typo is not treated as an error. The practical consequence is worth knowing: a misspelled key does not fail. It produces a project with no declared source folder, which then falls back to the whole project directory, and no libraries at all. If a project suddenly compiles the wrong files or cannot find a library class, check the spelling in the {\f1\fs18 .rfxproj} first. {\b The older German spellings still work.} These keys were {\f1\fs18 Quellen}, {\f1\fs18 Bibliothek} and {\f1\fs18 Typ} until 13.09.2026. Both are read, so a project file written before that goes on building; new files are written with the English ones.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Without a {\f1\fs18 Sources} line, the project directory itself is the source folder.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Why the file exists at all.} tsbRapidFX needs no build tool; the plugin compiles and builds by itself. But "no build tool" must not come to mean "buildable only in the IDE". Because the project is a {\i file} and not an IDE setting, {\f1\fs18 rfxc --project} builds exactly the same thing outside \u8212? on a server, reproducibly, under version control.\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 $ rfxc --project MyProgram.rfxproj\line 
written: /tmp/MyProgram/build/MyProgram.jar\line 
start it with: java -jar /tmp/MyProgram/build/MyProgram.jar\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj What it leaves behind:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 build/\line 
  MyProgram.jar          Main-Class and Class-Path in the manifest,\line 
                         the tsbRapidFX runtime packed inside\line 
  lib/\line 
    flatlaf-3.7.2.jar    copied beside it, named in Class-Path\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Move the two together.} The {\f1\fs18 Class-Path} entries are relative and resolve against the directory the jar sits in.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Because the jar is started by {\f1\fs18 java -jar}, everything that depends on the application class loader works normally \u8212? {\f1\fs18 ServiceLoader}, and therefore JDBC driver discovery, among them. That is not true of {\f1\fs18 --run}, and Chapter 12 says why.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Points to watch in a multi-file program:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Exactly one {\f1\fs18\b Sub Main}.} More is an error ({\f1\fs18 RFX0205}) and not a silent choice \u8212? otherwise the directory order would decide which program starts.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b The files are compiled in name order.} No message and no generated byte depends on the order the file system happens to list them in.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b Imports} is per file.} What one file imports, another does not see.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b There are no packages.} {\f1\fs18 Module Hello} becomes {\f1\fs18 Hello.class} at the root; two types of the same name are an error ({\f1\fs18 RFX0201}), even from different files.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The language server and the IDE\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 ./gradlew :lsp:jar\line 
java -jar lsp/build/libs/rfxlsp-0.1.0.jar\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 rfxlsp} speaks LSP over standard in and out, so any editor that knows the protocol \u8212? VS Code, Neovim, Emacs, IntelliJ through LSP4IJ \u8212? gets errors as you type, completion, hover, go-to-declaration and an outline.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b The server is thin, and that is the point.} Everything it answers comes out of {\f1\fs18 com.rapidfx.compiler.ide}, which knows no protocol; it answers in plain records. The server only converts character offsets into line and column. That is also why the same service can serve the designer with no protocol in between.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The IntelliJ plugin goes further: {\b the compiler runs inside the IDE.} No second process, no protocol, nothing to set up. The plugin compiles {\f1\fs18 compiler} and {\f1\fs18 runtime} into itself and calls {\f1\fs18 LanguageService} as an ordinary Java method. There is no second grammar either \u8212? the platform gets a flat token list, and every question about meaning goes to the compiler, so the editor cannot hold a different opinion from {\f1\fs18 rfxc}.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Compiled here, executed over there.} The compiler runs in the IDE because it is loaded anyway and has usually compiled the file already, so starting after typing costs nothing. The program itself gets a {\b JVM of its own}: it may open a window, call {\f1\fs18 System.exit} or run forever, and none of that may touch the IDE.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Auto-import works the way it does for Java: type {\f1\fs18 Files}, and the import appears. There are three routes \u8212? a suggestion while typing, Alt+Enter on a name already typed and shown in red, and unprompted insertion when there is exactly one candidate and {\i Add unambiguous imports on the fly} is on.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb400\sa140\f2\b\fs34\cf2 3 Basics: casing, types, variables, operators\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Casing\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Names are case-sensitive. Keywords are not.}\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Dim Total As Integer = 1\line 
Dim total As Integer = 2   ' two different variables\line 
\line 
dim x As Integer           ' allowed - dim, Dim and DIM are one word\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The line falls there because the two cases have nothing to do with each other.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Names} distinguish because Java lies underneath and every name there has exactly one spelling. A language that let {\f1\fs18 toUpperCase} through as {\f1\fs18 TOUPPERCASE} would write your code differently from the way it is called. And in daily use: a typo is then an unknown name rather than a silently different variable.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Keywords} do not distinguish, because nobody coming from VB6, Xojo or B4J ever wrote {\f1\fs18 If} with a capital I because they meant something by it \u8212? the editor did it. Insisting on the spelling would be a concession with nothing in return.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A word that means something in exactly one place stays an ordinary name everywhere else: {\f1\fs18 get}, {\f1\fs18 set}, {\f1\fs18 in}, {\f1\fs18 to}, {\f1\fs18 step}, {\f1\fs18 each}, {\f1\fs18 then}, {\f1\fs18 until}, {\f1\fs18 when}, {\f1\fs18 preserve} and {\f1\fs18 handles}. That has to be so, because {\f1\fs18 get}, {\f1\fs18 set} and {\f1\fs18 in} are also the names of the most-used members of the JDK. After a dot there is never a keyword anyway, only a member name \u8212? {\f1\fs18 names.get(0)} and {\f1\fs18 it.next()} are calls, not statements.\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 A consequence worth knowing early: {\f1\fs18 Class} is a keyword, so {\f1\fs18 Class.forName(...)} does not parse. Write {\f1\fs18 java.lang.Class.forName(...)}. This comes up when you load a JDBC driver by hand \u8212? see Chapter 12.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Statements and lines\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b A statement is a line.} A colon separates two statements on one line:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Dim a As Integer = 1 : Dim b As Integer = 2\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b A line continues} when it ends with an underscore, or when it ends with a comma, an operator or an open bracket:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Print "The sum is " + _\line 
      (a + b)\line 
\line 
Print Total(1,\line 
            2,\line 
            3)\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Comments} come in four spellings. {\f1\fs18 '}, {\f1\fs18 REM} and {\f1\fs18 //} run to the end of the line; {\f1\fs18 /* ... */} runs across lines.\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 ' This way\line 
REM And this way\line 
// And this way, if your fingers come from Java\line 
Dim x As Integer = 1   ' also after code\line 
Dim y As Integer = 2   // and so\line 
\line 
/*\line 
 * Across several lines, for what needs more than one sentence.\line 
 */\line 
Dim z As Integer = 4 /* even mid-expression */ * 3\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 {\b A block comment is whitespace, even across lines.} What was one statement stays one \u8212? the newline {\i inside} the comment does not count. The newline {\b after} the {\f1\fs18 */} ends the statement as always, which is why a comment block between two lines changes nothing. It does not nest: a {\f1\fs18 /*} inside a comment is text, as in C and Java. A missing {\f1\fs18 */} is {\f1\fs18 RFX0006} \u8212? otherwise the rest of the file would silently stop being code.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The built-in types\par
