\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 {\b {\f1\fs18\b Integer} is 32 bits}, not 16 as in VB6. If you need the old width, use {\f1\fs18 Short}. The reason is the JVM: it has no 16-bit arithmetic, so every operation would have to be masked afterwards \u8212? for a type nobody wants in new code.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Beyond these, {\b every Java class is a type}. See Chapter 9.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Variables\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Dim count As Integer          ' 0\line 
Dim name As String            ' "" - not Nothing\line 
Dim value As Double = 3.5\line 
Dim sum = 0                   ' type from the initial value: Integer\line 
Var counter As Integer = 1    ' Var means the same as Dim\line 
Const Vat As Double = 0.19    ' immutable\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b {\f1\fs18\b Var} and {\f1\fs18\b Dim} are the same word.} Two spellings for one idea, so that nobody has to unlearn a habit \u8212? coming from Basic you write {\f1\fs18 Dim}, coming from elsewhere you write {\f1\fs18 Var}.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Without an initial value a variable gets its type's default: numbers 0, {\f1\fs18 Boolean} {\f1\fs18 False}, {\f1\fs18 String} {\b {\f1\fs18\b ""}} (not {\f1\fs18 Nothing} \u8212? {\f1\fs18 s + "x"} must never fail on an empty variable), references {\f1\fs18 Nothing}, a {\f1\fs18 Structure} a fresh instance.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The type is fixed at the declaration. {\f1\fs18 Dim sum = 0} makes {\f1\fs18 sum} an {\f1\fs18 Integer}, and no later assignment changes that.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Writing numbers and text\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 42          ' Integer\line 
3000000000  ' Long - does not fit in Integer\line 
3.14        ' Double\line 
1.5E7       ' Double, 15000000\line 
1L          ' Long, even though it would fit in an Integer\line 
&HFF        ' hexadecimal, 255\line 
&O17        ' octal, 15\line 
&B1010      ' binary, 10\line 
"Text"      ' String\line 
"He said ""Hello"""    ' two quotes make one\line 
True / False\line 
Nothing     ' the null reference - Null and Nil spell the same thing\line 
#2024-01-15#          ' Date\line 
#2024-01-15 14:30#    ' Date with time\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
