\pard\plain\keepn\sb220\sa80\f2\b\i\fs22\cf1 Shifting bits\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 And}, {\f1\fs18 Or} and {\f1\fs18 Xor} are bitwise between integers and logical between booleans. Plus the three shifts:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Dim colour As Integer = &HFF00CC\line 
Print (colour >> 16) And 255      ' 255 - the red byte\line 
Print -16 >> 2                    ' -4  - arithmetic, the sign stays\line 
Print -16 >>> 28                  ' 15  - unsigned, zeros from the top\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The result takes the type of the {\b left} side: an {\f1\fs18 Integer} stays an {\f1\fs18 Integer} even when shifted by a {\f1\fs18 Long}. The count is masked to the width \u8212? five bits for {\f1\fs18 Integer}, six for {\f1\fs18 Long} \u8212? so {\f1\fs18 1 << 33} is {\f1\fs18 2} and not an error. Both are Java's rule and VB.NET's at once.\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 {\b An {\f1\fs18\b L} after a number makes it a {\f1\fs18\b Long}}, even when it would fit in an {\f1\fs18 Integer}. Without the suffix the magnitude decides. The difference shows up exactly here: {\f1\fs18 1 << 40} is {\f1\fs18 256} (masked to five bits), {\f1\fs18 1L << 40} is {\f1\fs18 1099511627776}.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb220\sa80\f2\b\i\fs22\cf1 Three places where tsbRapidFX does not compute like Java\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b {\f1\fs18\b /} is always floating point.} {\f1\fs18 3 / 2} is {\f1\fs18 1.5}, even between two {\f1\fs18 Integer}. For truncating division there is {\f1\fs18 \\}: {\f1\fs18 3 \\ 2} is {\f1\fs18 1}. That is VB's rule and it removes the most common arithmetic surprise a Basic programmer meets in a C-like language.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b {\f1\fs18\b ^} binds tighter than the sign.} {\f1\fs18 -2 ^ 2} is {\f1\fs18 -4}, not {\f1\fs18 4}.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b {\f1\fs18\b Not} binds looser than a comparison.} {\f1\fs18 Not a = b} means {\f1\fs18 Not (a = b)}. The same line would not even compile in Java.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb220\sa80\f2\b\i\fs22\cf1 Concatenating with +\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 +} concatenates as soon as one side is text, converting the other \u8212? the same rule as Java, Xojo and B4J:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Print "Value: " + 42 + " / " + True + " / " + 1.5\line 
' Value: 42 / true / 1.5\line 
\line 
Print 1 + 2          ' 3 - both sides numbers, so it adds\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 &} does the same and stays for the VB habit. It is the older spelling of one operation, not a second behaviour.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Both tolerate {\f1\fs18\b Nothing}.} A missing string concatenates to nothing instead of raising an error \u8212? and that matters more here than in VB, because every Java method is allowed to return {\f1\fs18 null}:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Dim missing As String = System.getProperty("does.not.exist")   ' Nothing\line 
Print "[" + missing + "]"      ' []\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb220\sa80\f2\b\i\fs22\cf1 = against Is\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 =} asks about the value, {\f1\fs18 Is} about identity:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Dim a As String = "x"\line 
Dim b As String = "x"\line 
Print a = b          ' True - same content\line 
Print a Is b         ' depends on whether it is the same object\line 
Print a Is Nothing   ' the usual "not there" test\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb220\sa80\f2\b\i\fs22\cf1 AndAlso and OrElse\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 And} and {\f1\fs18 Or} always evaluate both sides. {\f1\fs18 AndAlso} and {\f1\fs18 OrElse} stop as soon as the answer is settled \u8212? which you need when the right side is not even valid without the left:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 If f IsNot Nothing AndAlso f.Exists() Then\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
