\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Dim n As Integer = CInt(1.5)                    ' 1\line 
Dim d As Double = 7                             ' by itself\line 
Dim s As String = CStr(42)                      ' "42"\line 
Dim t As String = CType(list.get(0), String)    ' out of a collection\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The conversions work on text too: {\f1\fs18 CInt("42")} is {\f1\fs18 42} and reports nonsense as an error. For a forgiving reading, use {\f1\fs18 Val}.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Arrays\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Dim values(9) As Integer       ' ten elements, indices 0 to 9\line 
Dim empty() As String          ' no array yet, only the variable\line 
\line 
values(0) = 1\line 
Print values(0)\line 
Print values.Length            ' 10\line 
Print UBound(values)           ' 9  - the highest index\line 
Print LBound(values)           ' 0  - always\line 
\line 
Dim parts As String() = "a,b,c".split(",")\line 
Dim t As String\line 
For Each t In parts\line 
    Print t\line 
Next t\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 {\b The number in {\f1\fs18\b Dim values(9)} is the upper bound, not the count.} Nine means ten elements. That is VB's rule, and it is why {\f1\fs18 For i = 0 To 9} fits a VB array exactly.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj There are {\b no array literals}: {\f1\fs18 New Object() \{...\}} is not parsed. Arrays are filled by index, as in VB:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Dim columns(2) As Object\line 
columns(0) = "Number"\line 
columns(1) = "Name"\line 
columns(2) = "City"\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Multi-dimensional arrays do not exist yet ({\f1\fs18 RFX0900}).\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Print\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Print "a line"\line 
Print "no break";              ' semicolon at the end\line 
Print "a"; "b"                 ' a semicolon joins: ab\line 
Print "a", "b"                 ' a comma sets a tab\line 
Print                          ' blank line\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 Print} writes numbers in VB's spelling \u8212? {\f1\fs18 1} rather than {\f1\fs18 1.0}, {\f1\fs18 10000000} rather than {\f1\fs18 1.0E7}, and a date in the local form. Booleans, though, come out as Java's: {\b {\f1\fs18\b true} and {\f1\fs18\b false}}, lower case. The difference is deliberate. Number formatting is legibility for a human; a boolean gets compared, logged and written to files, and then it has to be exactly what {\f1\fs18 String.valueOf} produces on the same JVM.\par
\pard\sb160\sa160\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb400\sa140\f2\b\fs34\cf2 4 Control flow\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 If\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 If number > 100 Then\line 
    Print "large"\line 
ElseIf number > 10 Then\line 
    Print "medium"\line 
Else\line 
    Print "small"\line 
End If\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj On one line, without {\f1\fs18 End If}:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 If number < 0 Then Print "negative"\line 
If number < 0 Then Print "negative" Else Print "not negative"\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The condition must be {\f1\fs18 Boolean}. {\f1\fs18 If 1 Then} is an error ({\f1\fs18 RFX0303}) \u8212? a number is not a truth.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 If(condition, then, else)} returns a value and evaluates only the branch it chooses:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Print If(age >= 18, "adult", "minor")\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 IIf(...)} is the same expression under VB's name. Unlike VB6, where {\f1\fs18 IIf} was an ordinary function and evaluated {\i both} branches, it short-circuits here \u8212? which is exactly what made {\f1\fs18 IIf(x Is Nothing, "", x.Name)} crash there.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Select Case\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Select Case grade\line 
    Case 1\line 
        Print "very good"\line 
    Case 2, 3\line 
        Print "good to fair"\line 
    Case 4 To 5\line 
        Print "sufficient to poor"\line 
    Case Is > 5\line 
        Print "fail"\line 
    Case Else\line 
        Print "no grade"\line 
End Select\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Four shapes of {\f1\fs18 Case}, mixable within one branch:\par
\trowd\trgaph80\trleft0\trpaddl80\trpaddr80\trpaddt40\trpaddb40
\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clcbpat6\cellx4790
\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clcbpat6\cellx9580
