\pard\plain\page\keepn\sb0\sa280\qc\f2\b\fs52\cf2 Part II \u8212? Using every Java class\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb400\sa140\f2\b\fs34\cf2 9 Java is used, not rebuilt\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The whole Java ecosystem is open: the JDK, Swing, any jar from Maven Central. There is nothing to register, nothing to generate and no layer in between.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Finding types\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Imports java.util\line 
Imports javax.swing\line 
\line 
Dim list As ArrayList = New ArrayList()              ' via Imports\line 
Dim m As java.util.HashMap = New java.util.HashMap() ' fully qualified\line 
Dim sb As StringBuilder = New StringBuilder()        ' java.lang is always there\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 Imports} names {\b a Java package or a single class} \u8212? both, and the class spelled exactly as Java spells it:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Imports java.nio.file            ' the whole package\line 
Imports java.nio.file.Files      ' only this one class\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj There is no tsbRapidFX namespace system of its own. A language whose purpose is the JDK uses the JDK's naming, or every example from the internet would have to be translated first. That is precisely why the second form exists: every Java example starts with {\f1\fs18 import java.nio.file.Files;}, and you should be able to carry that across line for line.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj If both are named, {\b the explicitly named class wins}. {\f1\fs18 java.util.List} and {\f1\fs18 java.awt.List} are both called {\f1\fs18 List}; whoever writes one of them down means it.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj An {\f1\fs18 Imports} with neither a package nor a class behind it is reported as a {\b warning}, {\f1\fs18 RFX0610} \u8212? not an error, because a package can be real and merely missing right now, for instance when a library has not been built yet.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Nested classes} are written with a dot, not with the dollar sign that stands in the class file:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Dim e As java.util.Map.Entry = java.util.Map.entry("a", "b")\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Creating and using objects\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Dim file As java.io.File = New java.io.File("/tmp/x.txt")\line 
Print file.exists()\line 
Print file.getAbsolutePath()\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Static members through the type name:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Print Integer.MAX_VALUE\line 
Print Math.max(3, 7)\line 
Print Integer.parseInt("42")\line 
Print System.getProperty("java.version")\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 {\b {\f1\fs18\b Integer} is two things, and both are right.} As a {\i type} it is {\f1\fs18 int}, so {\f1\fs18 Dim n As Integer} allocates nothing. As the {\i receiver} of a static member it is {\f1\fs18 java.lang.Integer}, so {\f1\fs18 Integer.MAX_VALUE} stays reachable. The same holds for {\f1\fs18 Double}, {\f1\fs18 Boolean} and the rest.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Overloads\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Which version of a method is meant is decided by Java's rules \u8212? fixed arity without boxing, then with boxing, then varargs, and within a stage the most specific wins:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Dim sb As StringBuilder = New StringBuilder()\line 
sb.append("x=")      ' append(String)\line 
sb.append(42)        ' append(int)   - not append(Object)\line 
sb.append(1.5)       ' append(double)\line 
sb.append(True)      ' append(boolean)\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj That the {\f1\fs18 int} version wins is not merely tidy: the {\f1\fs18 Object} version would allocate an object for every number. If nothing fits, the message lists the signatures that exist ({\f1\fs18 RFX0602}).\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Varargs\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Print String.format("%s is %d year old", "tsbRapidFX", 1)\line 
Print String.format("no arguments")\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Properties in the VB style\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj If a Java class follows the JavaBean convention, its state reads and writes like a variable:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 window.Title = "tsbRapidFX"        ' calls setTitle\line 
Print window.Width                 ' calls getWidth\line 
Print button.Enabled               ' calls isEnabled\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The shortcut applies only when there is {\b no real field} of that name and the matching {\f1\fs18 get}/{\f1\fs18 is}/{\f1\fs18 set} method exists. So it never invents a member; it only writes an existing one the way VB writes it. {\f1\fs18 file.exists()} stays a method call \u8212? {\f1\fs18 exists} is a question, not a property.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Java method names are case-exact.} It is {\f1\fs18 signIn}, not {\f1\fs18 SignIn}. Only JavaBean {\i properties} get the VB spelling: {\f1\fs18 .Text}, {\f1\fs18 .Password}, {\f1\fs18 .Font}, {\f1\fs18 .ToolTipText}.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Generics are erased\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj tsbRapidFX has no type arguments. An {\f1\fs18 ArrayList} is a raw {\f1\fs18 ArrayList}, and what comes out is {\f1\fs18 Object}:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Dim list As ArrayList = New ArrayList()\line 
list.add("Hello")\line 
\line 
Dim s As String = CType(list.get(0), String)\line 
Print s.length()\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj That costs one {\f1\fs18 CType} at the point of extraction and nothing else. Putting things {\i in} boxes automatically: {\f1\fs18 list.add(5)} stores an {\f1\fs18 Integer}.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Arrays\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Arrays coming from Java behave like your own:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Dim parts As String() = "a,b,c".split(",")\line 
Print parts.Length\line 
Print parts(0)\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Interfaces and events\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Where a single-method interface is expected, write a lambda or an {\f1\fs18 AddressOf}:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 button.addActionListener(Sub(e)\line 
    Print "clicked"\line 
End Sub)\line 
\line 
button.addActionListener(AddressOf Handle)\line 
\line 
list.sort(Function(a, b) CType(a, String).compareTo(CType(b, String)))\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj And the other way round: a tsbRapidFX class may implement a Java interface, so that the JDK calls back into your code.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 What the compiler does with the class path\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj It {\b loads} the classes rather than reading the class files itself, so the JDK does the resolution across module path, jar and directory. A class therefore has to be loadable, which is the same condition the finished program is under anyway.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Nothing is ever initialised.} Naming a class starts nothing: {\f1\fs18 Imports com.formdev.flatlaf} installs no look and feel. {\f1\fs18 Class.forName} always runs with {\f1\fs18 initialize=false}.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 A jar from Maven Central\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Whatever is not in the JDK has to be on the class path:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 java -jar rfxc-0.1.0.jar \\\line 
     -cp lib/flatlaf-3.7.2.jar \\\line 
     --run window.rfx\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj After that it is like everything else:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Imports com.formdev.flatlaf\line 
\line 
FlatDarkLaf.setup()          ' from now on things are built dark\line 
FlatLaf.updateUI()           ' and what is already built follows\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\sb160\sa160\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb400\sa140\f2\b\fs34\cf2 10 Worked example: java.nio.file\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj This chapter is one package, followed all the way through, because {\f1\fs18 java.nio.file} shows every rule of Chapter 9 at once: a static-only class, a factory method, checked exceptions, varargs, an interface with erased generics, and a whole family of overloads.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The tsbRapidFX standard library already has {\f1\fs18 ReadAllText} and {\f1\fs18 WriteAllText} (Chapter 8). Everything below is the JDK's own, reached directly \u8212? which is the point: {\b there is no tsbRapidFX wrapper to wait for.}\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Files.readString and Files.writeString\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The Javadoc says:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 public static String readString(Path path) throws IOException\line 
public static Path writeString(Path path, CharSequence csq, OpenOption... options)\line 
       throws IOException\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj In tsbRapidFX:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Imports java.nio.file\line 
\line 
Module Files1\line 
    Sub Main()\line 
        Var target As Path = Path.of("/tmp/rfxman/notes.txt")\line 
        Files.writeString(target, "First line" + rfxNewLine + "Second line")\line 
        Var text As String = Files.readString(target)\line 
        Print text\line 
        Print "Characters: " + Len(text)\line 
        Files.deleteIfExists(target)\line 
    End Sub\line 
End Module\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Output:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 First line\line 
Second line\line 
Characters: 22\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Four things happened there, and none of them needed anything special:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b Path.of(...)}} is a static factory on an interface. Interfaces with static methods are ordinary receivers; write the name and call it.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b throws IOException} was ignored.} {\f1\fs18 throws} is a Java {\i compiler} rule, not a JVM one, and tsbRapidFX does not have it. The exception still flies if the file is missing \u8212? you catch it when you want to.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b Files.writeString(target, text)}} picked the two-argument overload, with the {\f1\fs18 OpenOption...} varargs empty.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b rfxNewLine}} is the standard library's line separator, and {\f1\fs18 +} concatenated a {\f1\fs18 String} with it.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The whole package, in one program\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Imports java.nio.file\line 
Imports java.nio.charset\line 
\line 
Module Files2\line 
    Sub Main()\line 
        Var dir As Path = Path.of("/tmp/rfxman/work")\line 
        Files.createDirectories(dir)\line 
\line 
        Var file As Path = dir.resolve("report.txt")\line 
        Files.writeString(file, "alpha" + rfxNewLine)\line 
        Files.writeString(file, "beta" + rfxNewLine, StandardOpenOption.APPEND)\line 
        Files.writeString(file, "gamma" + rfxNewLine, StandardCharsets.UTF_8, _\line 
                StandardOpenOption.APPEND)\line 
\line 
        Var line As Object\line 
        For Each line In Files.readAllLines(file)\line 
            Print "line: " + CStr(line)\line 
        Next line\line 
\line 
        Print "Exists: " + Files.exists(file)\line 
        Print "Size: " + Files.size(file)\line 
\line 
        Try\line 
            Print Files.readString(dir.resolve("missing.txt"))\line 
        Catch e As java.io.IOException\line 
            Print "not readable: " + e.getMessage()\line 
        End Try\line 
\line 
        Files.deleteIfExists(file)\line 
        Files.deleteIfExists(dir)\line 
    End Sub\line 
End Module\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Output:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 line: alpha\line 
line: beta\line 
line: gamma\line 
Exists: true\line 
Size: 17\line 
not readable: /tmp/rfxman/work/missing.txt\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Point by point:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b Files.writeString(file, text, StandardOpenOption.APPEND)}} \u8212? varargs need nothing; you simply pass the argument. {\f1\fs18 StandardOpenOption} is an enum in {\f1\fs18 java.nio.file}, so the {\f1\fs18 Imports} covers it.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b The three-and-more-argument overload} takes a {\f1\fs18 Charset} before the options. {\f1\fs18 StandardCharsets.UTF_8} needs {\f1\fs18 Imports java.nio.charset}, which is a second package and a second {\f1\fs18 Imports} line \u8212? that is all.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b Files.readAllLines} returns a {\f1\fs18\b List<String>}.} Generics are erased, so the loop variable is declared {\f1\fs18 As Object} and {\f1\fs18 CStr} turns it into text. Declaring it {\f1\fs18 As String} works too, and then the conversion happens for you:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Var line As String\line 
For Each line In Files.readAllLines(file)\line 
    Print "line: " + line\line 
Next line\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b Files.exists} returns a {\f1\fs18\b boolean}} and {\f1\fs18 Print} writes it as {\f1\fs18 true}, Java-style.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b Files.size} returns a {\f1\fs18\b long}} and prints as {\f1\fs18 17}, VB-style \u8212? no {\f1\fs18 .0}, no exponent.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b The {\f1\fs18\b Try/Catch}} catches {\f1\fs18 java.io.IOException}. {\f1\fs18 NoSuchFileException} is a subclass of it, so the broader branch is enough; a narrower one first would work too.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Streams and lambdas over a directory\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 Files.list}, {\f1\fs18 Files.walk} and {\f1\fs18 Files.lines} return a {\f1\fs18 java.util.stream.Stream}. It is {\f1\fs18 AutoCloseable}, so it belongs in a {\f1\fs18 Using}:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Imports java.nio.file\line 
\line 
Using entries As java.util.stream.Stream = Files.list(Path.of("/tmp"))\line 
    entries.forEach(Sub(p) Print CType(p, Path).getFileName())\line 
End Using\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Two rules from Chapter 9 meet here: the lambda fills the single-method interface {\f1\fs18 Consumer}, and because generics are erased its parameter arrives as {\f1\fs18 Object}, so {\f1\fs18 CType} says what you know it to be.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Byte content\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Var raw As Byte() = Files.readAllBytes(path)\line 
