\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The standard library is the short road for the common case. {\f1\fs18 java.nio.file} is the whole road, and it is never further away than an {\f1\fs18 Imports}.\par
\pard\sb160\sa160\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb400\sa140\f2\b\fs34\cf2 11 Reading Javadoc and writing tsbRapidFX\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Everything in the JDK is available to you, and so is everything on Maven Central. What is {\i not} available is a tsbRapidFX version of their documentation \u8212? the Java 21 runtime image alone holds {\b 28,094 classes}, 14,853 of them not counting nested ones. Nothing anyone could write would be more than a slice of that, and a slice that goes stale.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj So the skill worth having is reading Javadoc as it is. It is shorter than it feels: most of a Javadoc page already reads the way you would write it, and the places where it does not come to about fifteen rules.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Most of it reads straight across\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Javadoc:   public void setTitle(String title)\line 
tsbRapidFX: window.setTitle("Hello")\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Javadoc:   public int indexOf(String str)\line 
tsbRapidFX: Var at As Integer = text.indexOf("dampf")\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj No semicolons, no type in front of a call, and the argument types mean exactly what they say. If a Javadoc page shows you nothing but signatures, you can use it without knowing any of the rules below.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The one that really bites: generics\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Javadoc:   public interface List<E>\line 
           E get(int index)\line 
           boolean add(E e)\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj That {\f1\fs18 E} looks like a type you chose. In tsbRapidFX there is no type argument, so {\b {\f1\fs18\b get} gives you back an {\f1\fs18\b Object}} and you say what it is:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Imports java.util\line 
\line 
Var names As ArrayList = New ArrayList()\line 
names.add("World")\line 
\line 
Var first As String = CType(names.get(0), String)\line 
Print first.toUpperCase()\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Putting things {\i in} needs nothing: {\f1\fs18 add(E)} accepts anything, and a number is boxed on the way. Taking things {\i out} is where the cast goes.\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 {\b Why it is like this.} Generics are erased on the JVM: {\f1\fs18 List<String>} and {\f1\fs18 List<Integer>} are the same class at run time, and the type argument exists only in the Java compiler. tsbRapidFX does not carry one, so it tells you the truth \u8212? {\f1\fs18 Object} \u8212? instead of a promise it cannot keep.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
