\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Checked exceptions do not exist here\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Javadoc:   public static String readString(Path path) throws IOException\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Java forces you to catch that or declare it. tsbRapidFX does neither:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Imports java.nio.file\line 
\line 
Var text As String = Files.readString(Path.of("notes.txt"))\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj If the file is missing, the exception still flies; you catch it if you want to. {\b Read {\f1\fs18\b throws} as a hint} \u8212? it tells you what can go wrong, and you decide whether to handle it.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Extending a Java class\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Javadoc:   protected void paintComponent(Graphics g)\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 protected} means "for subclasses", and that is what it means here too:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Imports javax.swing\line 
Imports java.awt\line 
\line 
Public Class Canvas\line 
    Inherits JPanel\line 
\line 
    Public Overrides Sub paintComponent(g As Graphics)\line 
        MyBase.paintComponent(g)\line 
        g.drawString("drawn by tsbRapidFX", 20, 20)\line 
    End Sub\line 
End Class\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A base class whose constructor is {\f1\fs18 protected} \u8212? {\f1\fs18 java.util.TimerTask} is the classic \u8212? can still be inherited from. What you cannot do is {\f1\fs18 New} it from outside, which is exactly what Java means by it.\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 A base class with {\b no parameterless constructor at all} is a different matter: you must call {\f1\fs18 MyBase.New(...)} with arguments as the first statement, or the compiler reports {\f1\fs18 RFX0704}. That is worth remembering, because it is what stops some library base classes from being subclassed casually.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 What to ignore\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Type parameters} \u8212? {\f1\fs18 <E>}, {\f1\fs18 <K,V>}, {\f1\fs18 <? super T>}. Read them as {\f1\fs18 Object}.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b throws}} \u8212? a hint, see above.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b final}} on a parameter or a local. It says nothing about how you call the method.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b @Override}, {\f1\fs18\b @FunctionalInterface}} and other annotations. tsbRapidFX has no annotation syntax; nothing you write needs them.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Module names} ({\f1\fs18 java.base}, {\f1\fs18 java.desktop}) unless a library is not on your class path at all \u8212? and then it is a {\f1\fs18 -cp} question, not a code one.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The editor already does half of this\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Hover over a call and tsbRapidFX tells you the signature {\b in its own terms}, not Java's:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Function get(index As Integer) As Object\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj That line answers the generics question before you ask it. In the IDE this is faster than looking anything up \u8212? and it is the same information the compiler used, so it cannot disagree with what your program does.\par
