\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Pick the version you actually compile against. Which JDK that is depends on the JVM running the compiler, not on tsbRapidFX.\par
\pard\plain\page\keepn\sb0\sa280\qc\f2\b\fs52\cf2 Part III \u8212? Databases\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb400\sa140\f2\b\fs34\cf2 12 JDBC in tsbRapidFX\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj There is no database layer in tsbRapidFX, and there is not going to be one. {\f1\fs18 java.sql} is in the JDK, it is the interface every database in the world ships a driver for, and Chapter 9 already explained why using it is nothing special: {\f1\fs18 Connection}, {\f1\fs18 PreparedStatement} and {\f1\fs18 ResultSet} are ordinary Java types reached by ordinary calls.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj What this chapter adds is the shape a database program takes in Basic, the two places where tsbRapidFX surprises a Java programmer, and a worked connection for each of the usual databases.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The shape, once\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Imports java.sql\line 
\line 
Module Db\line 
    Sub Main()\line 
        Var url As String = "jdbc:h2:/tmp/shop"\line 
        Using c As Connection = DriverManager.getConnection(url, "sa", "")\line 
            Using s As Statement = c.createStatement()\line 
                s.executeUpdate("CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS customer (" + _\line 
                        "id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY, " + _\line 
                        "name VARCHAR(80) NOT NULL, city VARCHAR(80))")\line 
            End Using\line 
\line 
            Using ins As PreparedStatement = c.prepareStatement( _\line 
                    "INSERT INTO customer (name, city) VALUES (?, ?)")\line 
                ins.setString(1, "Anna Berger")\line 
                ins.setString(2, "Hamburg")\line 
                ins.executeUpdate()\line 
            End Using\line 
\line 
            Using q As PreparedStatement = c.prepareStatement( _\line 
                    "SELECT id, name, city FROM customer WHERE city = ? ORDER BY name")\line 
                q.setString(1, "Hamburg")\line 
                Using rows As ResultSet = q.executeQuery()\line 
                    Do While rows.next()\line 
                        Print rows.getInt("id") + " | " + rows.getString("name") _\line 
                                + " | " + rows.getString("city")\line 
                    Loop\line 
                End Using\line 
            End Using\line 
        End Using\line 
    End Sub\line 
End Module\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj That program was compiled and run against a real H2 database. Its output:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 1 | Anna Berger | Hamburg\line 
2 | Bert Cole | Hamburg\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Everything worth knowing is in it:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b Using} is the right shape for every JDBC object.} {\f1\fs18 Connection}, {\f1\fs18 Statement}, {\f1\fs18 PreparedStatement} and {\f1\fs18 ResultSet} are all {\f1\fs18 AutoCloseable}, so {\f1\fs18 Using} closes them however the block is left \u8212? including on an exception. That is the {\f1\fs18 try}-with-resources of Java, written in Basic, and it is the reason a tsbRapidFX database program leaks nothing.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Parameters are {\f1\fs18\b ?} and are set by 1-based index}, exactly as JDBC defines them. Never build SQL by concatenating user input; a {\f1\fs18 PreparedStatement} is both faster and the only defence against injection that actually holds.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b Do While rows.next()}} is the loop. {\f1\fs18 rows.next()} returns a {\f1\fs18 boolean}, so the condition is already the right type.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Column access by name} \u8212? {\f1\fs18 rows.getString("name")} \u8212? survives a change in the {\f1\fs18 SELECT} order. By index, {\f1\fs18 rows.getString(2)}, is faster and more brittle; pick per taste, but pick one.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b Print rows.getInt("id") + " | " + ...}} works because {\f1\fs18 +} concatenates once one side is text (Chapter 3).\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Transactions\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 c.setAutoCommit(False)\line 
Try\line 
    Using ins As PreparedStatement = c.prepareStatement( _\line 
            "INSERT INTO customer (name, city) VALUES (?, ?)")\line 
        ins.setString(1, "Anna Berger")\line 
        ins.setString(2, "Hamburg")\line 
        ins.addBatch()\line 
        ins.setString(1, "Bert Cole")\line 
        ins.setString(2, "Hamburg")\line 
        ins.addBatch()\line 
        ins.executeBatch()\line 
    End Using\line 
    c.commit()\line 
Catch e As SQLException\line 
    c.rollback()\line 
    Throw\line 
Finally\line 
    c.setAutoCommit(True)\line 
End Try\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 addBatch} / {\f1\fs18 executeBatch} is the difference between one round trip and a thousand. On a remote database it is usually the single largest speed-up available for an import.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Reading a result into your own objects\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Imports java.sql\line 
Imports java.util\line 
\line 
Public Class Customer\line 
    Public Property Id As Integer\line 
    Public Property Name As String\line 
    Public Property City As String\line 
End Class\line 
\line 
Public Class CustomerStore\line 
    Private url As String\line 
\line 
    Public Sub New(connectionUrl As String)\line 
        url = connectionUrl\line 
    End Sub\line 
\line 
    Public Function InCity(city As String) As ArrayList\line 
        Var found As ArrayList = New ArrayList()\line 
        Using c As Connection = DriverManager.getConnection(url)\line 
            Using q As PreparedStatement = c.prepareStatement( _\line 
                    "SELECT id, name, city FROM customer WHERE city = ? ORDER BY name")\line 
                q.setString(1, city)\line 
                Using rows As ResultSet = q.executeQuery()\line 
                    Do While rows.next()\line 
                        Var one As Customer = New Customer()\line 
                        one.Id = rows.getInt("id")\line 
                        one.Name = rows.getString("name")\line 
                        one.City = rows.getString("city")\line 
                        found.add(one)\line 
                    Loop\line 
                End Using\line 
            End Using\line 
        End Using\line 
        Return found\line 
    End Function\line 
End Class\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Reading them back out costs a {\f1\fs18 CType}, because the {\f1\fs18 ArrayList} is raw:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Var one As Object\line 
For Each one In store.InCity("Hamburg")\line 
    Print CType(one, Customer).Name\line 
Next one\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Auto-properties earn their keep here: {\f1\fs18 Public Property Name As String} generates the field and both accessors, so the class is three lines and Java sees a proper bean.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Nulls\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A SQL {\f1\fs18 NULL} comes back as {\f1\fs18 Nothing} for object types and as {\f1\fs18 0} / {\f1\fs18 False} for primitives \u8212? which is why {\f1\fs18 wasNull} exists:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Var amount As Double = rows.getDouble("amount")\line 
If rows.wasNull() Then\line 
    Print "no amount recorded"\line 
Else\line 
    Print FormatCurrency(amount)\line 
End If\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj For a text column, the {\f1\fs18 Nothing} check is enough, and {\f1\fs18 +} tolerates it (Chapter 3), so {\f1\fs18 "[" + rows.getString("city") + "]"} prints {\f1\fs18 []} rather than throwing.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Writing a null:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 ins.setNull(2, Types.VARCHAR)\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Dates\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A tsbRapidFX {\f1\fs18 Date} is a {\f1\fs18 java.time.LocalDateTime}, and modern JDBC drivers accept {\f1\fs18 java.time} directly through {\f1\fs18 getObject} / {\f1\fs18 setObject}:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Var due As Date = rows.getObject("due_at", GetType(java.time.LocalDateTime))\line 
ins.setObject(3, Now())\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 GetType(T)} is tsbRapidFX's way of writing Java's {\f1\fs18 T.class}. The older {\f1\fs18 rows.getTimestamp("due_at").toLocalDateTime()} works as well and is what you will find in most examples on the web.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Two things that will catch you\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj These are not database problems. They are two places where tsbRapidFX behaves differently from Java, and both show up for the first time when somebody writes their first JDBC program.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b 1. {\f1\fs18\b Class} is a keyword, so {\f1\fs18\b Class.forName} does not parse.}\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Class.forName("org.h2.Driver")            ' RFX0103: expected an expression\line 
java.lang.Class.forName("org.h2.Driver")  ' correct\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Keywords are case-insensitive in tsbRapidFX (Chapter 3), which means the word {\f1\fs18 Class} is taken at the start of a statement no matter how it is spelled. Writing the type out fully sidesteps it, because after a dot there is never a keyword.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b 2. {\f1\fs18\b rfxc --run} does not auto-discover drivers.}\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Since JDBC 4, {\f1\fs18 DriverManager} finds drivers through {\f1\fs18 ServiceLoader}. Under {\f1\fs18 --run} the program is loaded by a class loader of the compiler's making, and the service lookup does not reach the driver jar. You get:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 java.sql.SQLException: No suitable driver found for jdbc:h2:/tmp/shop\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Two remedies, and the second is the better one:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 ' Works under --run: register the driver by hand\line 
java.lang.Class.forName("org.h2.Driver")\line 
Var c As Connection = DriverManager.getConnection(url)\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 # Or compile and run the class normally, where ServiceLoader works as designed\line 
rfxc -cp lib/h2-2.4.240.jar -o out Db.rfx\line 
java -cp "out:rfxc-0.1.0.jar:lib/h2-2.4.240.jar" Db\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A built jar ({\f1\fs18 --jar}) or a project ({\f1\fs18 --project}) is started by {\f1\fs18 java -jar} and therefore has no such problem. {\b {\f1\fs18\b --run} is a convenience for quick tries; a database program should be built and started.}\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Connection pooling\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 DriverManager.getConnection} opens a real connection every time, and on a network database that is tens of milliseconds. For anything serving more than one user, put a pool in front of it. HikariCP is the usual choice and it is an ordinary jar:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Imports com.zaxxer.hikari\line 
\line 
Public Class Database\line 
    Private Shared pool As HikariDataSource\line 
\line 
    Public Shared Sub Open(url As String, user As String, secret As String)\line 
        Var config As HikariConfig = New HikariConfig()\line 
        config.setJdbcUrl(url)\line 
        config.setUsername(user)\line 
        config.setPassword(secret)\line 
        config.setMaximumPoolSize(20)\line 
        pool = New HikariDataSource(config)\line 
    End Sub\line 
\line 
    Public Shared Function Take() As java.sql.Connection\line 
        Return pool.getConnection()\line 
    End Function\line 
End Class\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 Using c As Connection = Database.Take()} then returns the connection to the pool at {\f1\fs18 End Using} instead of closing a socket.\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 {\b This is one of the rare things that genuinely belongs in a {\f1\fs18\b Shared} field}, even in a web application: a pool is infrastructure that every user shares alike, and it holds nobody's data. Chapter 19 draws that line precisely.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\sb160\sa160\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\par
