\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The driver class is only needed for the {\f1\fs18 java.lang.Class.forName} case above. Normally the URL is enough.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 SQLite \u8212? a file, and nothing to install\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Var url As String = "jdbc:sqlite:/var/lib/myapp/customers.db"\line 
Using c As Connection = DriverManager.getConnection(url)\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj In memory, for a test that must leave nothing behind:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Var url As String = "jdbc:sqlite::memory:"\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj SQLite is the right answer whenever the database belongs to the program rather than to an organisation: an appliance, a desktop application, a small web service on one machine. There is no server, no user, no password, and the whole thing is one file you can copy.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Two things to know. It takes a {\b write lock on the whole file}, so concurrent writers queue; turn on WAL mode if that starts to hurt:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Using s As Statement = c.createStatement()\line 
    s.execute("PRAGMA journal_mode = WAL")\line 
    s.execute("PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON")\line 
End Using\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The second: foreign keys are {\b off by default}, which surprises everyone once.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 H2 \u8212? a file or a server, and pure Java\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 ' A file, with the database staying open while the JVM lives\line 
Var url As String = "jdbc:h2:/tmp/shop;DB_CLOSE_DELAY=-1"\line 
Using c As Connection = DriverManager.getConnection(url, "sa", "")\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 ' In memory\line 
Var url As String = "jdbc:h2:mem:test;DB_CLOSE_DELAY=-1"\line 
\line 
' A server somewhere\line 
Var url As String = "jdbc:h2:tcp://localhost:9092/~/shop"\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj H2 has no native part at all, so the jar runs on every platform your program does \u8212? the same property that made FlatLaf the look and feel of choice. It also speaks a large part of PostgreSQL's dialect, which makes it a good stand-in for tests of a program that will run on PostgreSQL.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 PostgreSQL\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Var url As String = "jdbc:postgresql://db.example.com:5432/shop"\line 
Using c As Connection = DriverManager.getConnection(url, "shopuser", secret)\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj With options in the URL, which is where PostgreSQL likes them:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Var url As String = "jdbc:postgresql://db.example.com:5432/shop" _\line 
        + "?ssl=true&sslmode=require&ApplicationName=Kundenverwaltung"\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 RETURNING} is worth knowing, because it replaces a second round trip:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Using ins As PreparedStatement = c.prepareStatement( _\line 
        "INSERT INTO customer (name, city) VALUES (?, ?) RETURNING id")\line 
    ins.setString(1, "Anna Berger")\line 
    ins.setString(2, "Hamburg")\line 
    Using rows As ResultSet = ins.executeQuery()\line 
        If rows.next() Then Print "new id: " + rows.getInt(1)\line 
    End Using\line 
End Using\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 MySQL and MariaDB\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 ' MySQL\line 
Var url As String = "jdbc:mysql://db.example.com:3306/shop" _\line 
        + "?useUnicode=true&characterEncoding=UTF-8&serverTimezone=UTC"\line 
\line 
' MariaDB - same shape, different scheme\line 
Var url As String = "jdbc:mariadb://db.example.com:3306/shop"\line 
\line 
Using c As Connection = DriverManager.getConnection(url, "shopuser", secret)\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 On MySQL, ask for {\f1\fs18 utf8mb4} in the schema rather than {\f1\fs18 utf8}. The older name is three bytes wide and cannot store an emoji or several perfectly ordinary CJK characters \u8212? a defect that surfaces months later as a truncated row.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Generated keys, the portable way:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Using ins As PreparedStatement = c.prepareStatement( _\line 
        "INSERT INTO customer (name, city) VALUES (?, ?)", _\line 
        Statement.RETURN_GENERATED_KEYS)\line 
    ins.setString(1, "Anna Berger")\line 
    ins.setString(2, "Hamburg")\line 
    ins.executeUpdate()\line 
    Using keys As ResultSet = ins.getGeneratedKeys()\line 
        If keys.next() Then Print "new id: " + keys.getLong(1)\line 
    End Using\line 
End Using\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj That form works on MySQL, MariaDB, H2, SQLite and SQL Server alike, and it is the one to reach for when a program has to be portable.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Microsoft SQL Server\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Var url As String = "jdbc:sqlserver://db.example.com:1433" _\line 
        + ";databaseName=shop;encrypt=true;trustServerCertificate=false"\line 
Using c As Connection = DriverManager.getConnection(url, "shopuser", secret)\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj With Windows integrated security, and no user or password in the call:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Var url As String = "jdbc:sqlserver://db.example.com:1433" _\line 
        + ";databaseName=shop;integratedSecurity=true;encrypt=true"\line 
Using c As Connection = DriverManager.getConnection(url)\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Note the semicolons: SQL Server separates its URL options with {\f1\fs18 ;}, not {\f1\fs18 &}. Since driver version 10 {\f1\fs18 encrypt} defaults to {\f1\fs18 true}, which is why a connection that worked for years may start failing after an upgrade with a certificate complaint rather than a login one.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Oracle\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 ' Service name - the modern form\line 
Var url As String = "jdbc:oracle:thin:@//db.example.com:1521/SHOPPDB"\line 
\line 
' SID - the older form, still everywhere\line 
Var url As String = "jdbc:oracle:thin:@db.example.com:1521:SHOP"\line 
\line 
Using c As Connection = DriverManager.getConnection(url, "shopuser", secret)\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Oracle's {\f1\fs18 ojdbc11} jar is not on Maven Central under a permissive licence in the way the others are; it comes from Oracle's own repository and carries their terms. That is a licensing matter, not a technical one \u8212? once the jar is on the class path, nothing in the code differs.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Derby and HSQLDB \u8212? embedded, in pure Java\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 ' Derby, creating the database on first use\line 
Var url As String = "jdbc:derby:/var/lib/myapp/shop;create=true"\line 
\line 
' HSQLDB, a file\line 
Var url As String = "jdbc:hsqldb:file:/var/lib/myapp/shop;shutdown=true"\line 
Using c As Connection = DriverManager.getConnection(url, "SA", "")\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Both are alternatives to H2 with the same virtue: no native library, so one jar serves every platform.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Firebird\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Var url As String = "jdbc:firebirdsql://db.example.com:3050/shop?encoding=UTF8"\line 
Using c As Connection = DriverManager.getConnection(url, "SYSDBA", secret)\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Building it all\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Whatever the database, the build is the same two steps:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 rfxc -cp "lib/*" -o out src/*.rfx\line 
java -cp "out:rfxc-0.1.0.jar:lib/*" Main\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj or, as a project:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Name       = Kundenverwaltung\line 
Sources = src\line 
Library = lib/postgresql-42.7.4.jar\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 rfxc --project Kundenverwaltung.rfxproj\line 
java -jar build/Kundenverwaltung.jar\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The driver jar is named in the manifest as {\f1\fs18 Class-Path} and stays in {\f1\fs18 lib/} beside the built jar \u8212? not copied inside it. Move the two together.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Using a database for sign-in\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj For a web application the credentials usually come from a database, and tsbWEB has that already. {\f1\fs18 tsbWebJdbcAuth} takes a supplier of connections and two queries:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Var users As tsbWebJdbcAuth = New tsbWebJdbcAuth( _\line 
        Sub() java.sql.DriverManager.getConnection("jdbc:sqlite:/var/lib/tsbweb/users.db"), _\line 
        "SELECT display_name, password_hash FROM users WHERE login = ?", _\line 
        "SELECT role FROM user_roles WHERE login = ?")\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The first argument is a lambda filling a single-method interface (Chapter 5). The queries are yours so that an existing user table does not have to be rebuilt. Passwords are checked with {\f1\fs18 PBKDF2WithHmacSHA256}, 210,000 rounds, salted per user \u8212? about 83 ms per attempt, out of the JDK, with no extra dependency. {\b The password is verified in this process and never sent to the database.} An unknown user costs the same time as a wrong password, because otherwise the difference would be a way to read out the user list.\par
\pard\plain\page\keepn\sb0\sa280\qc\f2\b\fs52\cf2 Part IV \u8212? User interface\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb400\sa140\f2\b\fs34\cf2 14 Swing in tsbRapidFX\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Swing is the interface toolkit for tsbRapidFX. Not one of several \u8212? the only one.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The reason is distribution. Swing is in the JDK: one jar, every platform, no {\f1\fs18 lib/} directory of native parts. JavaFX ships different jars per platform ({\f1\fs18 mac-aarch64}, {\f1\fs18 win}, ...), so a JavaFX program only runs on the platform it was built on. For a system whose promise is "build a jar and hand it over", that settled it.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The second reason is that the language fits. tsbRapidFX maps {\f1\fs18 btn.Text = "x"} onto {\f1\fs18 getText}/{\f1\fs18 setText} \u8212? JavaBeans. Swing {\i is} a JavaBean library. JavaFX wants {\f1\fs18 textProperty().bind(...)}, for which tsbRapidFX has no syntax.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 A window, by hand\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Imports javax.swing\line 
Imports java.awt\line 
\line 
Public Class CounterWindow\line 
    Private frame As JFrame\line 
    Private display As JLabel\line 
    Private state As Integer = 0\line 
\line 
    Public Sub New()\line 
        frame = New JFrame("tsbRapidFX")\line 
        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE)\line 
        frame.setSize(400, 200)\line 
\line 
        Var content As JPanel = New JPanel()\line 
        content.setLayout(New GridLayout(2, 1, 8, 8))\line 
\line 
        display = New JLabel("State: 0", SwingConstants.CENTER)\line 
\line 
        Var button As JButton = New JButton("+1")\line 
        button.addActionListener(Sub(e)\line 
            state += 1\line 
            display.Text = "State: " + state\line 
        End Sub)\line 
\line 
        With content\line 
            .add(display)\line 
            .add(button)\line 
        End With\line 
\line 
        frame.setContentPane(content)\line 
    End Sub\line 
\line 
    Public Sub Show()\line 
        frame.Visible = True\line 
    End Sub\line 
End Class\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Three tsbRapidFX habits are visible there and worth naming:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b display.Text = ...}} is {\f1\fs18 setText}. The JavaBean shortcut (Chapter 9) is what keeps generated form code from being a wall of setter calls.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b The lambda captures {\f1\fs18\b state} and {\f1\fs18\b display}}, which are fields of the instance. Under the hood the body becomes a static method and the captured values become parameters; you never see that.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b With content}} evaluates the receiver once and saves repeating it.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Look and feel\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Swing's own look is Metal, and everybody replaces it in the first hour. FlatLaf is the one the templates bring:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Imports com.formdev.flatlaf\line 
\line 
Sub Main()\line 
    FlatLightLaf.setup()          ' before the first window\line 
    SwingUtilities.invokeLater(Sub()\line 
        New CounterWindow().Show()\line 
    End Sub)\line 
End Sub\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Two rules, and forgetting either one is the usual cause of a half-themed window:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Set it before the first window is built.} {\f1\fs18 UIManager} decides only what is built {\i from now on}.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Switching at run time takes two calls.} {\f1\fs18 FlatDarkLaf.setup()} tells the {\f1\fs18 UIManager}; {\f1\fs18 FlatLaf.updateUI()} makes the windows already built follow. One alone is not enough.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj FlatLaf is a single jar under Apache 2.0, identical on every platform. The {\f1\fs18 flatlaf-intellij-themes} jar adds around forty ported IntelliJ themes.\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 {\b A look and feel is process-wide.} All windows share it, and \u8212? this matters in Chapter 21 \u8212? all {\i sessions} of a web server share it too. Per-user theming is not possible.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Threading\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Swing is single-threaded: components are touched on the event dispatch thread and nowhere else.\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 ' Start the interface on the EDT\line 
SwingUtilities.invokeLater(Sub()\line 
    New CounterWindow().Show()\line 
End Sub)\line 
\line 
' From a worker thread back onto the EDT\line 
SwingUtilities.invokeLater(Sub() display.Text = "done")\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Long work does not belong in an event handler on either target. On the desktop it freezes the window; on the web it blocks that user's session thread (Chapter 21). Start a thread:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Var worker As java.lang.Thread = New java.lang.Thread(Sub()\line 
    Var answer As String = LongQuery()\line 
    SwingUtilities.invokeLater(Sub() display.Text = answer)\line 
End Sub)\line 
worker.start()\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 What is in the box\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Everything {\f1\fs18 javax.swing} has, because it {\i is} {\f1\fs18 javax.swing}. Beyond the JDK, two libraries were settled on for tsbRapidFX and both are in the designer's catalogue:\par
