\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Desktop: opening a window and closing another\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A window is a {\f1\fs18 JFrame}. Opening the next one and letting the current one go is two lines:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Private Sub OnOpenDetail(e As ActionEvent)\line 
    Var detail As DetailWindow = New DetailWindow()\line 
    detail.Show()\line 
    frame.dispose()\line 
End Sub\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Order matters. Show the new window {\b first}, then dispose the old one. The other way round there is an instant in which no window is showing, and on some platforms that is enough for the application to lose focus or, if it was the last window, to end.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The whole of it, both directions:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Imports javax.swing\line 
Imports java.awt\line 
Imports java.awt.event\line 
\line 
Module Windows\line 
    Sub Main()\line 
        SwingUtilities.invokeLater(Sub()\line 
            New MainWindow().Show()\line 
        End Sub)\line 
    End Sub\line 
End Module\line 
\line 
Public Class MainWindow\line 
    Private frame As JFrame = New JFrame("Main")\line 
\line 
    Public Sub New()\line 
        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE)\line 
        frame.setSize(420, 240)\line 
        frame.setLocationRelativeTo(Nothing)\line 
\line 
        Var toDetail As JButton = New JButton("Open details")\line 
        toDetail.addActionListener(AddressOf OnOpenDetail)\line 
\line 
        Var panel As JPanel = New JPanel()\line 
        panel.add(toDetail)\line 
        frame.setContentPane(panel)\line 
    End Sub\line 
\line 
    Public Sub Show()\line 
        frame.Visible = True\line 
    End Sub\line 
\line 
    Private Sub OnOpenDetail(e As ActionEvent)\line 
        Var detail As DetailWindow = New DetailWindow()\line 
        detail.Show()\line 
        frame.dispose()\line 
    End Sub\line 
End Class\line 
\line 
Public Class DetailWindow\line 
    Private frame As JFrame = New JFrame("Details")\line 
\line 
    Public Sub New()\line 
        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.DISPOSE_ON_CLOSE)\line 
        frame.setSize(420, 240)\line 
        frame.setLocationRelativeTo(Nothing)\line 
\line 
        Var back As JButton = New JButton("Back")\line 
        back.addActionListener(Sub(e)\line 
            New MainWindow().Show()\line 
            frame.dispose()\line 
        End Sub)\line 
\line 
        Var panel As JPanel = New JPanel()\line 
        panel.add(back)\line 
        frame.setContentPane(panel)\line 
    End Sub\line 
\line 
    Public Sub Show()\line 
        frame.Visible = True\line 
    End Sub\line 
End Class\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b The close operation is the setting people get wrong.}\par
\trowd\trgaph80\trleft0\trpaddl80\trpaddr80\trpaddt40\trpaddb40
\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clcbpat6\cellx4790
\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clcbpat6\cellx9580
\pard\plain\intbl\sb20\sa20\f0\fs19\cf1\b Constant\cell
\pard\plain\intbl\sb20\sa20\f0\fs19\cf1\b What the close button does\cell
\row
\trowd\trgaph80\trleft0\trpaddl80\trpaddr80\trpaddt40\trpaddb40
\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\cellx4790
\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\cellx9580
\pard\plain\intbl\sb20\sa20\f0\fs19\cf1\b0 {\f1\fs18 JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE}\cell
\pard\plain\intbl\sb20\sa20\f0\fs19\cf1\b0 ends the whole program\cell
\row
\trowd\trgaph80\trleft0\trpaddl80\trpaddr80\trpaddt40\trpaddb40
\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\cellx4790
\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\cellx9580
\pard\plain\intbl\sb20\sa20\f0\fs19\cf1\b0 {\f1\fs18 JFrame.DISPOSE_ON_CLOSE}\cell
\pard\plain\intbl\sb20\sa20\f0\fs19\cf1\b0 releases this window; the program lives on if another window remains\cell
\row
\trowd\trgaph80\trleft0\trpaddl80\trpaddr80\trpaddt40\trpaddb40
\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\cellx4790
\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\cellx9580
\pard\plain\intbl\sb20\sa20\f0\fs19\cf1\b0 {\f1\fs18 JFrame.HIDE_ON_CLOSE}\cell
\pard\plain\intbl\sb20\sa20\f0\fs19\cf1\b0 hides it; the window stays in memory and can be shown again\cell
\row
\trowd\trgaph80\trleft0\trpaddl80\trpaddr80\trpaddt40\trpaddb40
\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\cellx4790
\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\cellx9580
\pard\plain\intbl\sb20\sa20\f0\fs19\cf1\b0 {\f1\fs18 JFrame.DO_NOTHING_ON_CLOSE}\cell
\pard\plain\intbl\sb20\sa20\f0\fs19\cf1\b0 nothing \u8212? you handle it in a {\f1\fs18 WindowListener}\cell
\row
\pard\sb0\sa140

\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Exactly one window in a program should carry {\f1\fs18 EXIT_ON_CLOSE}, and it should be the one whose closing genuinely means "I am finished". Every secondary window gets {\f1\fs18 DISPOSE_ON_CLOSE}. Putting {\f1\fs18 EXIT_ON_CLOSE} on all of them is the reason a program sometimes ends when a report window is closed.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 dispose()} releases the window and its native peer. {\f1\fs18 setVisible(False)} merely hides it, so the window and everything in it stay in memory \u8212? which is what you want if the user will come back to it, and a leak if they will not.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Asking before closing.} {\f1\fs18 WindowListener} has seven methods, so it is not a single-method interface and a lambda will not fill it. Inherit from {\f1\fs18 WindowAdapter} instead, which implements the other six as empty bodies, and override the one you want:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Public Class ClosingGuard\line 
    Inherits java.awt.event.WindowAdapter\line 
\line 
    Private frame As JFrame\line 
\line 
    Public Sub New(owner As JFrame)\line 
        frame = owner\line 
    End Sub\line 
\line 
    Public Overrides Sub windowClosing(e As java.awt.event.WindowEvent)\line 
        Var answer As Integer = JOptionPane.showConfirmDialog(frame, _\line 
                "Discard unsaved changes?", "Close", JOptionPane.YES_NO_OPTION)\line 
        If answer = JOptionPane.YES_OPTION Then frame.dispose()\line 
    End Sub\line 
End Class\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.DO_NOTHING_ON_CLOSE)\line 
frame.addWindowListener(New ClosingGuard(frame))\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Desktop: standard dialogs\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 JOptionPane} covers most of what a program asks:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 ' A message\line 
JOptionPane.showMessageDialog(frame, "Saved.", "Done", _\line 
        JOptionPane.INFORMATION_MESSAGE)\line 
\line 
' A question\line 
Var answer As Integer = JOptionPane.showConfirmDialog(frame, "Really delete?", _\line 
        "Question", JOptionPane.YES_NO_OPTION, JOptionPane.WARNING_MESSAGE)\line 
If answer = JOptionPane.YES_OPTION Then\line 
    Delete()\line 
End If\line 
\line 
' A line of text; Cancel gives Nothing\line 
Var name As String = JOptionPane.showInputDialog(frame, "Your name?", "Sign in", _\line 
        JOptionPane.QUESTION_MESSAGE)\line 
If name IsNot Nothing Then\line 
    Print "Hello, " + name\line 
End If\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The first argument is the parent, and it decides which window the dialog centres over and which window it blocks. Passing {\f1\fs18 Nothing} centres on the screen.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The standard library's {\f1\fs18 MsgBox} and {\f1\fs18 InputBox} (Chapter 8) are the same thing in VB's spelling, with one extra courtesy: {\b with no display they fall back to the console} instead of throwing, so a program with a message does not die on a build server.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Desktop: a dialog of your own\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj For anything with more than one field, build a {\f1\fs18 JDialog}. Created modal, {\f1\fs18 setVisible(True)} {\b blocks} until the dialog closes \u8212? which is what lets you write the answer-handling on the next line:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Private Sub OnEdit(e As ActionEvent)\line 
    Var dialog As JDialog = New JDialog(frame, "Customer", True)   ' True = modal\line 
    Var field As JTextField = New JTextField("Anna Berger", 20)\line 
\line 
    Var ok As JButton = New JButton("OK")\line 
    ok.addActionListener(Sub(ev)\line 
        result = field.Text\line 
        dialog.dispose()\line 
    End Sub)\line 
\line 
    Var cancel As JButton = New JButton("Cancel")\line 
    cancel.addActionListener(Sub(ev)\line 
        result = ""\line 
        dialog.dispose()\line 
    End Sub)\line 
\line 
    Var body As JPanel = New JPanel()\line 
    body.add(field)\line 
    body.add(ok)\line 
    body.add(cancel)\line 
\line 
    dialog.setContentPane(body)\line 
    dialog.pack()\line 
    dialog.setLocationRelativeTo(frame)\line 
    dialog.Visible = True          ' blocks here\line 
\line 
    Print "answer: " + result      ' runs after the dialog closed\line 
End Sub\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Four details:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b New JDialog(frame, "Customer", True)}} \u8212? the third argument is modality. Without it the dialog is modeless, {\f1\fs18 setVisible} returns immediately, and the line after it runs before the user has answered.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b The result travels in a field}, because a lambda can write a field of the enclosing instance but cannot return a value to the code after {\f1\fs18 setVisible}.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b pack()}} sizes the dialog to its content's preferred size. Use it instead of {\f1\fs18 setSize} for a dialog whose contents depend on translated text.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b setLocationRelativeTo(frame)}} centres over the parent. {\f1\fs18 Nothing} centres over the screen.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A {\b designer-drawn form} goes into a dialog just as well \u8212? that is the usual case in a real program:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Var form As CustomerForm = New CustomerForm()\line 
Var dialog As JDialog = New JDialog(frame, "Customer", True)\line 
dialog.setContentPane(form.GetRootPane())\line 
dialog.pack()\line 
dialog.setLocationRelativeTo(frame)\line 
dialog.Visible = True\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Web: there are no windows\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj On a server there is no {\f1\fs18 JFrame}. {\f1\fs18 new JFrame()} throws {\f1\fs18 HeadlessException} in the {\f1\fs18 Window} constructor, before any code of yours runs, and no amount of arrangement changes that: a {\f1\fs18 Window} needs a screen.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj So the web target replaces the {\i idea} of a window rather than the class. {\b The browser is the window}, and what you swap is what the browser shows.\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 {\b There is no {\f1\fs18\b tsbWebFrame} in the shipped libraries.} An early design sketched one for hand-written windows; the built product does not have it. Everything below is the road that exists.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The mechanism is one function and one call:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Public Function createRoot(session As tsbWebSession) As Component\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 createRoot} runs {\b once per session and again on every screen change}, on the session thread, with the session already in reach. Whatever it returns is an ordinary Swing component. To change screens, write into the session and ask for a rebuild:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Private Sub OnOpenDetails(e As ActionEvent)\line 
    Screen = "details"                ' a SessionStatic - see Chapter 20\line 
    tsbWebSession.rebuild()\line 
End Sub\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj There is {\b no router and no navigation}. {\f1\fs18 createRoot} decides afresh:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Public Class ScreenApp\line 
    Implements tsbWebApp\line 
\line 
    Public Function title() As String\line 
        Return "Screens"\line 
    End Function\line 
\line 
    Public Function createRoot(session As tsbWebSession) As Component\line 
        If Not session.SignedIn Then Return New SignInScreen().GetRootPane()\line 
        If "details".equals(WebWindows.Screen) Then\line 
            Return New DetailScreen().GetRootPane()\line 
        End If\line 
        Return New MainScreen().GetRootPane()\line 
    End Function\line 
End Class\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Three things about that, all of which matter:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b The rebuild happens {\i after} the handler returns, never in the middle of it.} A handler that touches the old screen on its way out therefore cannot leave half a page behind.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b A new instance per session, never a shared one.} Two users editing the same {\f1\fs18 JTextField} would watch each other type.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Signing in needs no code at all.} After a successful {\f1\fs18 signIn} the server notices the change and rebuilds by itself, so the handler has nothing to do on the success path.\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Private Sub OnSignIn(e As ActionEvent)\line 
    If Not tsbWebSession.signIn(login.Text, secret.Password) Then\line 
        hint.Text = "Sign-in failed"\line 
    End If\line 
    ' On success: nothing. The server rebuilds.\line 
End Sub\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 tsbWebSession.signOut()} signs out and rebuilds in the same way.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Web: standard dialogs\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 tsbWebOptionPane} has the same method names, the same constants and the same return values as {\f1\fs18 JOptionPane}. Ported code changes one word:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Var answer As Integer = tsbWebOptionPane.showConfirmDialog( _\line 
        Nothing, "Really delete?", "Question", False)\line 
\line 
If answer = tsbWebOptionPane.YES_OPTION Then\line 
    Delete()\line 
    tsbWebOptionPane.showMessageDialog(Nothing, "Done.", "Finished")\line 
End If\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
