\pard\sb0\sa140

\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The fourth argument of {\f1\fs18 showConfirmDialog} is {\b {\f1\fs18\b withCancel}}: {\f1\fs18 False} gives Yes and No, {\f1\fs18 True} adds Cancel. There are two- and three-argument overloads as well, matching {\f1\fs18 JOptionPane}'s.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A caller that only checks for {\f1\fs18 YES_OPTION} therefore treats a dismissal as a no \u8212? which is the safe direction, and the one {\f1\fs18 JOptionPane} has always taken.\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 {\b The buttons are labelled in German} \u8212? {\i Ja}, {\i Nein}, {\i Abbrechen} \u8212? in the version shipped at the time of writing. For an English-facing application, build the dialog yourself with {\f1\fs18 tsbWebDialog} (below), where the labels are yours.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b It blocks.} The line after {\f1\fs18 showConfirmDialog} runs only once the user has answered. Behind that is a nested event loop on the session thread \u8212? the same construction Swing's EDT uses to serve a modal {\f1\fs18 JDialog}. The waiting thread keeps taking work off its own queue, so the click carrying the answer arrives, the button fires, and the loop ends.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Modality is enforced, not suggested.} While a dialog is open, events aimed at anything underneath it are dropped at the server. Not greyed out and hoped for.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The {\f1\fs18 parent} argument is accepted and ignored. On a desktop it decides which window to centre over; here there is one page.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 showInputDialog} exists too, with {\f1\fs18 (parent, message)}, {\f1\fs18 (parent, message, preset)} and {\f1\fs18 (parent, message, preset, title)}. Check that order against your ported line: it is {\i preset then title}, which is not where {\f1\fs18 JOptionPane} puts them in every one of its overloads.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Why {\f1\fs18 JOptionPane} needs replacing at all is narrow: its own {\f1\fs18 show...} methods build a {\f1\fs18 JDialog}, and a {\f1\fs18 JDialog} is a {\f1\fs18 Window}, and a {\f1\fs18 Window} cannot exist without a screen. Everything below that \u8212? the panel, the buttons, the layout \u8212? is ordinary Swing and is built the ordinary way.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Web: a dialog of your own\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 tsbWebDialog} takes a component and a title, and {\f1\fs18 showModal()} returns whatever {\f1\fs18 close(...)} was given:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Private Sub OnEdit(e As ActionEvent)\line 
    Var form As CustomerDialog = New CustomerDialog("Anna Berger")\line 
    Var dialog As tsbWebDialog = New tsbWebDialog(form.GetRootPane(), "Customer")\line 
    form.Attach(dialog)\line 
\line 
    Var answer As Object = dialog.showModal()      ' blocks here\line 
    If answer Is Nothing Then\line 
        Print "cancelled"\line 
    Else\line 
        Print "answer: " + CType(answer, String)\line 
    End If\line 
End Sub\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Public Class CustomerDialog\line 
    Private root As JPanel = New JPanel()\line 
    Private field As JTextField\line 
    Private owner As tsbWebDialog\line 
\line 
    Public Sub New(preset As String)\line 
        field = New JTextField(preset, 20)\line 
\line 
        Var ok As JButton = New JButton("OK")\line 
        ok.addActionListener(Sub(e) owner.close(field.Text))\line 
\line 
        Var cancel As JButton = New JButton("Cancel")\line 
        cancel.addActionListener(Sub(e) owner.close())\line 
\line 
        root.add(field)\line 
        root.add(ok)\line 
        root.add(cancel)\line 
    End Sub\line 
\line 
    Public Sub Attach(dialog As tsbWebDialog)\line 
        owner = dialog\line 
    End Sub\line 
\line 
    Public Function GetRootPane() As JPanel\line 
        Return root\line 
    End Function\line 
End Class\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Compare that with the desktop version above and the difference is exactly two things: the class name, and that the answer comes back from {\f1\fs18 showModal()} instead of out of a field. The second is an improvement \u8212? {\f1\fs18 close(value)} hands the result up directly, so there is no shared field to keep in step.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b showModal()} must run on the session thread.} From anywhere else it throws {\f1\fs18 IllegalStateException} with the thread's name in the message, because a dialog needs a queue to pump and there is none elsewhere. In practice this means: call it from an event handler, not from a worker thread you started.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b close()} with no argument} returns {\f1\fs18 Nothing}, which is the natural reading of Cancel.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b The content is an ordinary component tree}, so a designer-drawn form does just as well as a hand-built panel.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Web: files in and out\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The web target adds one thing the desktop does not need, because a browser cannot reach the server's disk:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 ' Out\line 
tsbWebFiles.send(reportBytes, "Report.pdf", "application/pdf")\line 
tsbWebFiles.send("Number;Name" + rfxCrLf + "1;Anna", "Customers.csv")\line 
\line 
' In - blocks, like JFileChooser.showOpenDialog\line 
Var content As Byte() = tsbWebFiles.ask(".csv")\line 
If content IsNot Nothing Then\line 
    Load(content, tsbWebFiles.lastName())\line 
End If\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 ask} returns {\f1\fs18 Nothing} when the user cancelled. The download link is 256 bits, belongs to one session, is valid {\b once}, and expires after five minutes.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Menus, on both targets\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Menus are ordinary Swing and work unchanged in the browser, accelerators included:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Var bar As JMenuBar = New JMenuBar()\line 
Var file As JMenu = New JMenu("File")\line 
\line 
Var save As JMenuItem = New JMenuItem("Save")\line 
save.setAccelerator(KeyStroke.getKeyStroke( _\line 
        java.awt.event.KeyEvent.VK_S, java.awt.event.InputEvent.CTRL_DOWN_MASK))\line 
save.addActionListener(AddressOf OnSave)\line 
file.add(save)\line 
\line 
file.addSeparator()\line 
file.add(New JCheckBoxMenuItem("Show grid", True))\line 
bar.add(file)\line 
\line 
screen.add(bar, BorderLayout.NORTH)\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Submenus, separators, check items and radio items all travel. A context menu attaches as usual: {\f1\fs18 field.setComponentPopupMenu(popup)}.\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 On the web, {\b the modifiers travel raw}. The server might say Command because it is a Mac; a user on Windows has to read Ctrl. The browser labels and recognises them for {\i its} platform.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 What the web cannot do\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Named plainly, because finding out later is worse:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b The focus lives in the browser.} {\f1\fs18 requestFocus()} from the program has no effect.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b javax.swing.Timer} fires on the real EDT} and is not currently redirected to the session thread. For anything repeating, start a thread of your own \u8212? it inherits the session automatically.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b The look and feel is process-wide.} All sessions share it; per-user theming is not possible.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b A long handler blocks its session} \u8212? exactly as on the desktop, and with the same remedy.\par
\pard\sb160\sa160\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb400\sa140\f2\b\fs34\cf2 16 JTable \u8212? the full way\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 JTable} is the table when you need sorting, renderers, mixed column types or a model of your own. It is the JDK's, so everything written about it anywhere applies here unchanged.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The shortest complete table\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Imports javax.swing\line 
Imports javax.swing.table\line 
Imports java.awt\line 
\line 
Public Class TableDemo\line 
\line 
    Private grid As JTable\line 
    Private model As DefaultTableModel\line 
\line 
    Public Function Build() As JComponent\line 
        Var columns(2) As Object\line 
        columns(0) = "Id"\line 
        columns(1) = "Name"\line 
        columns(2) = "City"\line 
\line 
        model = New DefaultTableModel(columns, 0)\line 
        grid = New JTable(model)\line 
        grid.setAutoCreateRowSorter(True)\line 
        grid.setSelectionMode(ListSelectionModel.SINGLE_SELECTION)\line 
        grid.setRowHeight(22)\line 
        grid.getSelectionModel().addListSelectionListener(AddressOf OnRowPicked)\line 
\line 
        Var row(2) As Object\line 
        row(0) = Integer.valueOf(1)\line 
        row(1) = "Anna Berger"\line 
        row(2) = "Hamburg"\line 
        model.addRow(row)\line 
\line 
        Return New JScrollPane(grid)\line 
    End Function\line 
End Class\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Points that are tsbRapidFX rather than Swing:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b Var columns(2) As Object}} gives {\i three} slots, indices 0 to 2. The number in the brackets is the upper bound (Chapter 3). There are no array literals, so the columns are filled by index.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b Integer.valueOf(1)}} rather than plain {\f1\fs18 1}, because {\f1\fs18 DefaultTableModel} wants an {\f1\fs18 Object[]} and the boxed form says so plainly. Passing {\f1\fs18 1} boxes it for you as well; writing it out is clearer about what the array holds.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b New JScrollPane(grid)}} is not optional. A bare {\f1\fs18 JTable} draws no column headers \u8212? the header is a separate component that only a scroll pane puts in place.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Selection\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Private Sub OnRowPicked(e As javax.swing.event.ListSelectionEvent)\line 
    If e.getValueIsAdjusting() Then Return\line 
    Var picked As Integer = grid.getSelectedRow()\line 
    If picked < 0 Then Return\line 
    Var actual As Integer = grid.convertRowIndexToModel(picked)\line 
    Print "picked: " + CStr(model.getValueAt(actual, 1))\line 
End Sub\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Three guards, and each of them is a real bug if you leave it out:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b getValueIsAdjusting()}} is true while the mouse is still down, so a drag across five rows fires five times. Return early on it and you get one event per selection.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b picked < 0}} means nothing is selected. Clearing a selection also fires the listener.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b convertRowIndexToModel}} is the one people forget. With {\f1\fs18 setAutoCreateRowSorter(True)} the visible order is not the model order, so row 0 on screen may be row 47 in the model. Reading the model with the view index gives the wrong record \u8212? silently, and only after somebody clicks a column header.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Your own model\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 DefaultTableModel} stores {\f1\fs18 Object}s and knows nothing about your data. For real data, extend {\f1\fs18 AbstractTableModel} and let it read the list you already have:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Imports javax.swing.table\line 
Imports java.util\line 
\line 
Public Class CustomerModel\line 
    Inherits AbstractTableModel\line 
\line 
    Private rows As ArrayList = New ArrayList()\line 
\line 
    Public Sub SetRows(source As ArrayList)\line 
        rows = source\line 
        fireTableDataChanged()\line 
    End Sub\line 
\line 
    Public Overrides Function getRowCount() As Integer\line 
        Return rows.size()\line 
    End Function\line 
\line 
    Public Overrides Function getColumnCount() As Integer\line 
        Return 3\line 
    End Function\line 
\line 
    Public Overrides Function getColumnName(column As Integer) As String\line 
        Select Case column\line 
            Case 0\line 
                Return "Id"\line 
            Case 1\line 
                Return "Name"\line 
            Case 2\line 
                Return "City"\line 
        End Select\line 
        Return ""\line 
    End Function\line 
\line 
    Public Overrides Function getColumnClass(column As Integer) As java.lang.Class\line 
        If column = 0 Then Return GetType(java.lang.Integer)\line 
        Return GetType(String)\line 
    End Function\line 
\line 
    Public Overrides Function getValueAt(row As Integer, column As Integer) As Object\line 
        Var one As Customer = CType(rows.get(row), Customer)\line 
        Select Case column\line 
            Case 0\line 
                Return java.lang.Integer.valueOf(one.Id)\line 
            Case 1\line 
                Return one.Name\line 
            Case 2\line 
                Return one.City\line 
        End Select\line 
        Return ""\line 
    End Function\line 
\line 
    Public Overrides Function isCellEditable(row As Integer, column As Integer) As Boolean\line 
        Return column > 0\line 
    End Function\line 
\line 
    Public Overrides Sub setValueAt(value As Object, row As Integer, column As Integer)\line 
        Var one As Customer = CType(rows.get(row), Customer)\line 
        If column = 1 Then one.Name = CStr(value)\line 
        If column = 2 Then one.City = CStr(value)\line 
        fireTableCellUpdated(row, column)\line 
    End Sub\line 
End Class\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b {\f1\fs18\b getColumnClass} is what earns the effort.} It is how the table knows to right-align numbers, draw a {\f1\fs18 Boolean} as a checkbox and a {\f1\fs18 LocalDate} in the locale's format \u8212? none of which {\f1\fs18 DefaultTableModel} can do, because it stores everything as {\f1\fs18 Object}.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 GetType(T)} is tsbRapidFX's {\f1\fs18 T.class} (Chapter 3). Note {\f1\fs18 java.lang.Integer} written out: as a {\i type} the bare word {\f1\fs18 Integer} means {\f1\fs18 int}, which is not a class object.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Renderers\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A renderer decides how one cell looks. Because it is a single-method interface, a lambda fills it:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Imports javax.swing.table\line 
Imports java.awt\line 
\line 
Var money As TableCellRenderer = Function(table, value, selected, focused, row, column)\line 
    Var label As JLabel = New JLabel(FormatCurrency(CDbl(value)))\line 
    label.setHorizontalAlignment(SwingConstants.RIGHT)\line 
    label.setOpaque(True)\line 
    If selected Then\line 
        label.setBackground(table.getSelectionBackground())\line 
        label.setForeground(table.getSelectionForeground())\line 
    End If\line 
    Return label\line 
End Function\line 
\line 
grid.getColumnModel().getColumn(3).setCellRenderer(money)\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj For anything used on thousands of rows, prefer extending {\f1\fs18 DefaultTableCellRenderer} and reconfiguring the one component it already returns \u8212? building a fresh {\f1\fs18 JLabel} per cell is the usual reason a large table scrolls badly.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Sorting and filtering\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Var sorter As TableRowSorter = New TableRowSorter(model)\line 
grid.setRowSorter(sorter)\line 
\line 
' Sort by name, ascending\line 
Var keys As ArrayList = New ArrayList()\line 
keys.add(New javax.swing.RowSorter.SortKey(1, javax.swing.SortOrder.ASCENDING))\line 
sorter.setSortKeys(keys)\line 
\line 
' Filter as the user types\line 
search.getDocument().addDocumentListener(New SearchWatcher(sorter, search))\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A {\f1\fs18 Comparator} for a column is a lambda:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 sorter.setComparator(2, Function(a, b) _\line 
        CType(a, String).compareToIgnoreCase(CType(b, String)))\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Column widths\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 grid.setAutoResizeMode(JTable.AUTO_RESIZE_OFF)\line 
grid.getColumnModel().getColumn(0).setPreferredWidth(60)\line 
grid.getColumnModel().getColumn(1).setPreferredWidth(240)\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 JTable}'s own resizing spreads spare width {\b evenly} rather than in proportion to the preferred widths, which is why setting preferred widths and leaving auto-resize on does not do what it looks like it should. Switching it off and computing the pixels is the only way a proportion means a proportion \u8212? and that is exactly what {\f1\fs18 tsbSmartTable} does for you in the next chapter.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 In the browser\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A {\f1\fs18 JTable} works in a tsbWEB application unchanged, and it travels as {\b structure, not as a picture}: the browser builds a real table you can select in, sort and walk with the arrow keys.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj It is also {\b virtualised}. Ten thousand rows are no problem \u8212? the server sends a window of about 200 rows around whatever the user is looking at and adds more as they scroll. Un-windowed it would be some 695 kB of cells and a field 200,000 pixels tall.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The cell text comes from {\b the renderer}, not the raw value, so a {\f1\fs18 LocalDate} appears as {\f1\fs18 2026-09-05} and your own {\f1\fs18 TableCellRenderer} still decides how an amount looks. Sorting is done by Swing's own {\f1\fs18 RowSorter}, your {\f1\fs18 Comparator} included; the browser only reports which header was hit.\par
\pard\sb160\sa160\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb400\sa140\f2\b\fs34\cf2 17 tsbSmartTable \u8212? the short way\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 tsbSmartTable} is a table you fill by throwing strings at it. It sits {\b beside} {\f1\fs18 JTable}, not instead of it: when you need sorting, renderers, mixed column types or a model of your own, take the {\f1\fs18 JTable}. This one is for the other nine times out of ten.\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 {\b On the name.} The class is {\f1\fs18 tsbSmartTable}; in the designer's palette it appears as {\b SmartTable}. It lives in {\f1\fs18 tsbswing-1.0.jar}, is written in tsbRapidFX itself, and is compiled by {\f1\fs18 rfxc} \u8212? the designer generates the language its own library is written in.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The whole idea\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 table.Columns = "Name, Age, City"\line 
table.Add("Ada")\line 
table.Add("36")\line 
table.Add("London")\line 
table.Reload()\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The headers decide how many columns there are, so three headers make every three values a row. {\b Nothing else has to be declared, and there is no model to build.} A {\f1\fs18 JTable}'s model is in there, but it is not yours to touch.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj That one flat list is the whole trick. Throwing values in one after another is how a table is actually filled \u8212? out of a query, out of a file, out of a loop \u8212? and having to build a row object first is the step that makes {\f1\fs18 JTable} feel heavy.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 A worked example\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Imports javax.swing\line 
Imports java.awt\line 
\line 
Public Class QuickDemo\line 
\line 
    Private quick As tsbSmartTable\line 
\line 
    Public Function Build() As JComponent\line 
        quick = New tsbSmartTable()\line 
        quick.Columns = "Id, Name, City"\line 
        quick.ColumnWidths = "60, 50%, 50%"\line 
        quick.EditableColumns = "1, 2"\line 
\line 
        quick.AddRow(1, "Anna Berger", "Hamburg")\line 
        quick.AddRow(2, "Bert Cole", "Bremen")\line 
\line 
        quick.Add(3)\line 
        quick.Add("Cem Demir")\line 
        quick.Add("Kiel")\line 
\line 
        quick.Reload()\line 
\line 
        Print "rows: " + quick.RowCount()\line 
        Print "cell: " + quick.Value(0, 1)\line 
        Return quick\line 
    End Function\line 
End Class\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Output:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 rows: 3\line 
cell: Anna Berger\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 Add} takes an {\f1\fs18 Object}, not a {\f1\fs18 String}, on purpose: a table is mostly filled out of a query or a loop, and half of what goes in is a number. Having to write {\f1\fs18 CStr} around every one of them would be exactly the ceremony this class exists to remove. {\f1\fs18 AddRow} is a {\f1\fs18 ParamArray} for when you have the values together anyway.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Fill first, show once.} {\f1\fs18 Reload} empties the display and puts out everything currently in the list \u8212? a thousand rows are one event rather than a thousand. Ten thousand rows take about 2 ms.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Straight out of a query\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj This is what the class is for:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Imports java.sql\line 
\line 
Public Sub Load(c As Connection, city As String)\line 
    quick.Clear()\line 
    Using q As PreparedStatement = c.prepareStatement( _\line 
            "SELECT id, name, city FROM customer WHERE city = ? ORDER BY name")\line 
        q.setString(1, city)\line 
        Using rows As ResultSet = q.executeQuery()\line 
            Do While rows.next()\line 
                quick.Add(rows.getInt("id"))\line 
                quick.Add(rows.getString("name"))\line 
                quick.Add(rows.getString("city"))\line 
            Loop\line 
        End Using\line 
    End Using\line 
    quick.Reload()\line 
End Sub\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Compare that with the {\f1\fs18 AbstractTableModel} of Chapter 16 \u8212? a class, eight overrides and a list of objects \u8212? and the trade is plain. You give up column classes, renderers and sorting; you get eleven lines.\par
