\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Column widths that mean something\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Two kinds, and they mix:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 quick.ColumnWidths = "50%, 25%, 25%"     ' shares - each keeps its fraction as the window changes\line 
quick.ColumnWidths = "160, 60, 200"      ' pixels - fixed, and they stay fixed\line 
quick.ColumnWidths = "200, 50%, 50%"     ' the first is fixed, the other two share what is left\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Shares are the useful kind: a form that can be resized has no business pinning a name column to 160 pixels. {\b The numbers need not add up to a hundred} \u8212? what counts is their ratio, so {\f1\fs18 "1, 2, 1"} is a quarter, a half and a quarter. Empty means an equal share for every column; a zero or an unreadable entry leaves that column alone.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj This is the one thing {\f1\fs18 tsbSmartTable} does that a plain {\f1\fs18 JTable} will not do for you. {\f1\fs18 JTable}'s own resizing spreads spare width evenly rather than in proportion, so {\f1\fs18 "1%, 2%, 1%"} on a raw table comes out as three equal columns \u8212? measured, not assumed. {\f1\fs18 tsbSmartTable} switches auto-resize off and computes the pixels at layout time, giving the rounding remainder to the last share so the columns add up to the table exactly instead of leaving a one-pixel gap at the right.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj It is also the only table whose column widths the designer can set.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Editing, and reading the edits back\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj An edit writes straight back into the same flat list you filled, so reading the changes out afterwards is the same call as putting them in:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 quick.EditableColumns = "1, 2"\line 
' ... the user types ...\line 
Print quick.Value(0, 1)          ' one cell\line 
Var everything As ArrayList = quick.Values()   ' all of it, in order\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj There is no second copy to keep in step. That is a deliberate difference from the {\f1\fs18 JTable} route, where the model and your object list are two things and keeping them together is your problem.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 What is underneath\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Worth knowing, because it explains the two rough edges:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b It inherits from {\f1\fs18\b JScrollPane}} with a {\f1\fs18 JTable} inside, and it sets the column header explicitly in its constructor. A bare {\f1\fs18 JTable} picks its header up only once it has been added to a window \u8212? {\f1\fs18 JTable} does it from {\f1\fs18 addNotify} \u8212? so a table painted without ever being shown, which is exactly what a designer canvas does, would otherwise have no heading at all.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b There is a {\f1\fs18\b TableModel} in there.} "No model" is a promise about the {\i API}, not about the construction: a {\f1\fs18 JTable} without a model does not exist, and hand-drawing a grid would mean rebuilding selection, keyboard handling, scrolling and editing.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Because it inherits from {\f1\fs18 JScrollPane}, you do {\b not} wrap it in one:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 panel.add(quick)                    ' right\line 
panel.add(New JScrollPane(quick))   ' wrong - a scroll pane in a scroll pane\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Reaching the JTable inside\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj For anything the wrapper does not cover \u8212? selection listeners, renderers, a sorter \u8212? {\f1\fs18 Table()} hands out the real {\f1\fs18 JTable}, and it behaves like one:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 quick.Table().setSelectionMode(ListSelectionModel.SINGLE_SELECTION)\line 
quick.Table().getSelectionModel().addListSelectionListener(AddressOf OnPicked)\line 
quick.Table().setAutoCreateRowSorter(True)\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Once you find yourself using {\f1\fs18 Table()} for three different things, that is the signal to move to the {\f1\fs18 JTable} route of Chapter 16.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Which one to use\par
