\pard\plain\page\keepn\sb0\sa280\qc\f2\b\fs52\cf2 Part V \u8212? Web applications\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb400\sa140\f2\b\fs34\cf2 18 Writing a web application\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b You write Swing.} There is no web-only component, no annotation, no markup and no template language. The same forms show the same screens on a desktop if you hand them to a {\f1\fs18 JFrame} instead of to a server.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The server is {\f1\fs18 tsbWEB}. What it does with your Swing:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Swing computes the geometry.} Layout managers and font metrics run without a screen, and the browser gets finished coordinates. {\b No layout is rebuilt in CSS.}\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b The look and feel paints every component itself, as SVG.} FlatLaf's tick, its dropdown arrow, its slider knob \u8212? not approximated, drawn. Vector, not a bitmap: sharp when zoomed, and small.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Real HTML elements sit on top} wherever something is typed, chosen or scrolled. A {\f1\fs18 JTextField} gets its border painted and a real {\f1\fs18 <input>} inside it, with caret, selection and input method.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Tables, lists and trees travel as data}, not as a picture. The browser builds a real table you can select in, sort and walk with the arrow keys.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The three building blocks\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b 1. {\f1\fs18\b tsbWebApp} \u8212? one decision.}\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Public Class Application\line 
    Implements tsbWebApp\line 
\line 
    Public Function title() As String\line 
        Return "My web project"\line 
    End Function\line 
\line 
    Public Function createRoot(session As tsbWebSession) As Component\line 
        If Not session.SignedIn Then Return New SignInForm().GetRootPane()\line 
        If "customers".equals(Screen) Then Return New CustomerForm().GetRootPane()\line 
        Return New MainForm().GetRootPane()\line 
    End Function\line 
End Class\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 createRoot} runs {\b once per session and again on every screen change}, on the session thread, with the session already in reach. What it returns is an ordinary Swing component. For a form from the designer that is exactly {\f1\fs18 GetRootPane()}; {\f1\fs18 ShowIn} is never used, because it expects a {\f1\fs18 JFrame} and here the browser is the window.\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 {\b A new instance per session, never a shared one.} Two users editing the same {\f1\fs18 JTextField} would watch each other type.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b 2. {\f1\fs18\b tsbWebSession} \u8212? what belongs to one user.} Reachable from anywhere, with no parameter and no static variable. Chapter 19 covers it in full.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b 3. {\f1\fs18\b rebuild()} \u8212? the screen change.} There is no router. Write into the session, ask for a rebuild, and {\f1\fs18 createRoot} decides afresh. Chapter 15 covers it in full.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The entry point\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Imports com.tsbweb.server\line 
Imports com.tsbweb.session\line 
\line 
Module Main\line 
    Sub Main()\line 
        Var config As tsbWebConfig = New tsbWebConfig()\line 
        config.Port = 8099\line 
        config.Titel = "Customer management"\line 
        config.Thema = "FlatLightLaf"\line 
        config.LeerlaufMinuten = 30\line 
\line 
        Var users As tsbWebFixedAuth = tsbWebFixedAuth.builder() _\line 
                .user("anna", "Anna Berger", "secret", java.util.Set.of("admin")) _\line 
                .build()\line 
\line 
        tsbWebStart.Go(config, New Application(), users)\line 
    End Sub\line 
End Module\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj One line, and the program is a server you can double-click. {\f1\fs18 tsbWebStart.Go} does four things in a fixed order, and {\b three of them fail silently in the wrong one}:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 1.\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b java.awt.headless} first.} {\f1\fs18 GraphicsEnvironment} answers that question once and remembers. A manifest cannot set a system property, so a double-clicked jar has nowhere else to put it, and on a machine with no display AWT would not start at all.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 2.\tab {\b The configuration file second}, because the theme may be in it. Reading a properties file touches no AWT.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 3.\tab {\b The look and feel third.} {\f1\fs18 UIManager} is process-wide, and a component keeps the appearance it was built with.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 4.\tab {\b The server last}, and then the main thread stays put \u8212? otherwise the program ends and takes the server with it.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 tsbWebStart.Serve(...)} does the same and returns instead of waiting. Without sign-in, {\f1\fs18 tsbWebStart.Go(config, New Application())} is enough.\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 {\f1\fs18 config.Thema} is {\b not decoration}: the server paints every component with this look and feel and sends the drawing, so this is what the browser shows. Pick a theme in the designer and this line is updated for you when the form is saved \u8212? preview and server cannot drift apart.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 What an operator may change\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A file {\b {\f1\fs18\b tsbweb.properties} beside the jar} overrides what the program set:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 port                = 8443\line 
titel               = Customer management North\line 
thema               = dunkel\line 
zertifikat          = /etc/tsbweb/key.p12\line 
zertifikat.passwort = secret\line 
leerlauf.minuten    = 20\line 
sitzungen.max       = 750     # without this line: no ceiling\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Only keys that are present take effect, so a file with one line changes one thing. At start-up the output says {\b what was taken}:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Customer management North running on http://localhost:8443/\line 
  Idle 20 min\line 
  from tsbweb.properties: \{port=8443, thema=dunkel, titel=Customer management North\}\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Otherwise somebody who edited the file and sees nothing happen could not tell a typo from a file in the wrong directory. {\b A typo is named, not swallowed:} {\f1\fs18 port = eightthousand} reports itself and the built-in value stays.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The certificate password belongs in the environment variable {\f1\fs18 TSBWEB_ZERT_PASSWORT} rather than in the file \u8212? it is read first. A password in a file survives a backup, a copy and a glance over the shoulder; if it has to be there, then with {\f1\fs18 chmod 600}. The start-up output never shows the value, only {\f1\fs18 (set)}.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 HTTP or HTTPS \u8212? never both\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj There are {\b two methods and no switch}:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 tsbWebServer.http(8099, New Application(), users)\line 
\line 
tsbWebServer.https(8443, New Application(), users, _\line 
        java.nio.file.Path.of("/etc/tsbweb/key.p12"), password)\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Which server is running decides whether the session cookie is marked {\f1\fs18 Secure}. There is no second opinion that can be wrong. The old arrangement \u8212? a flag beside the port \u8212? allowed two failures that only show up in production: an unencrypted server with a {\f1\fs18 Secure} cookie (the browser never sends it back, nothing works, nobody can see why), and an encrypted server without it (the session id travels in clear the moment a user meets an {\f1\fs18 http://} link). The same reasoning governs the {\f1\fs18 Strict-Transport-Security} header.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Whoever needs both starts {\b two servers} \u8212? and then knows they have two.\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 keytool -genkeypair -alias tsbweb \\\line 
        -keyalg RSA -keysize 2048 -validity 365 \\\line 
        -dname "CN=localhost, O=Your house, C=DE" \\\line 
        -storetype PKCS12 \\\line 
        -keystore key.p12 \\\line 
        -storepass YOURPASSWORD -keypass YOURPASSWORD\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj PKCS#12 or JKS \u8212? the type is detected from the file, not configured, so there is nothing to set wrongly. {\f1\fs18 https(...)} {\b overwrites the {\f1\fs18\b char[]} it was given before it returns}, which is why it takes a {\f1\fs18 char[]} and not a {\f1\fs18 String}: a string cannot be overwritten and lies around until the garbage collector takes pity on it. TLS versions and cipher suites stay with the JDK, whose defaults are reviewed with every Java release; a list written down here would be a list nobody maintains.\par
