\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj That is all of it. There are no per-page routes and no router: which screen a user sees is decided by {\f1\fs18 createRoot}.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Why SSE and not WebSocket.} {\f1\fs18 com.sun.net.httpserver} offers {\b no protocol upgrade} \u8212? {\f1\fs18 HttpExchange} does not hand out the socket. A WebSocket would mean a second server on a second port with a hand-written frame codec. Server-sent events need none of that: an ordinary response that is never closed. Measured overhead is about 14 ms per event, and 200 simultaneous streams hold without trouble.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Creating a project\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj In IntelliJ: {\b New Project \u8594? RapidFX \u8212? Web}. The wizard writes three {\f1\fs18 .rfx} files, copies four jars into {\f1\fs18 lib/} and adds the project file, {\f1\fs18 tsbweb.properties}, a README and a {\f1\fs18 .gitignore}. No Maven, no Gradle, no network \u8212? whoever creates a project has everything needed to compile and start it.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Two of the three files are {\b drawn in the designer}. Right-click \u8594? {\b Open in Designer} (Chapter 23).\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj By hand:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 java -jar rfxc.jar --project MyProject.rfxproj\line 
java -jar build/MyProject.jar\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Or without a project file:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 java -jar rfxc.jar -cp "lib/*" -o out MyProgram.rfx\line 
java -Djava.awt.headless=true -cp "out:lib/*" Main\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Pitfalls, named\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b No static field for user data.} Harmless on the desktop, a data breach on the web: the second person to sign in reads the first one's. Chapter 20 is entirely about this.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b A long handler blocks its session} \u8212? exactly as on the desktop. For long work, start a thread; it inherits the session automatically.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b The look and feel is process-wide.} All sessions share it.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b {\f1\fs18\b javax.swing.Timer} fires on the real EDT} and is not redirected to the session thread. Use a thread for anything repeating.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b The focus lives in the browser.} {\f1\fs18 requestFocus()} from the program has no effect.\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Var worker As java.lang.Thread = New java.lang.Thread(Sub()\line 
    ' tsbWebSession is reachable here - the scope is inheritable\line 
    Var answer As String = LongQuery()\line 
    display.Text = answer\line 
End Sub)\line 
worker.start()\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\sb160\sa160\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb400\sa140\f2\b\fs34\cf2 19 Variables: where a value lives and how long\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Every variable in tsbRapidFX answers two questions: {\b who can see it}, and {\b whose value is it}. On the desktop the second question has one answer and nobody notices it. On a server it has as many answers as there are users, and getting it wrong is the single most expensive mistake in the whole system.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj This chapter lays out all five kinds side by side. The next chapter takes the fifth one apart.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The five kinds\par
