\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Locals and parameters\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Sub Count()\line 
    Dim i As Integer          ' 0\line 
    Dim name As String        ' "" - not Nothing\line 
    Var total = 0             ' type from the initial value: Integer\line 
    Const Vat As Double = 0.19\line 
End Sub\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A local lives from its declaration to the end of the procedure and dies with the call. Two calls, two values, even on the same thread \u8212? this is the only kind of variable that needs no thought at all, which is why it should be your first choice for everything.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Its type is fixed at the declaration. {\f1\fs18 Dim sum = 0} makes {\f1\fs18 sum} an {\f1\fs18 Integer} and no later assignment changes that.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A {\f1\fs18 For} variable declared in the header exists only inside the loop:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 For i As Integer = 1 To 3\line 
    Print i\line 
Next i\line 
' i is gone here\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 The generated {\f1\fs18 LocalVariableTable} covers the whole method rather than the exact scope, so a {\i debugger} shows names outside their block. That is debug information only; the compiler still enforces the scope.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b A local hides a field of the same name}, and a parameter is a local. That rule is what makes the constructor idiom work:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Public Sub New(owner As String)\line 
    Me.owner = owner          ' Me. reaches the field the parameter hides\line 
End Sub\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Instance fields\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Public Class Account\line 
    Private owner As String\line 
    Private balance As Double = 0\line 
End Class\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj One value per object. A field with an initial value is set {\b in every constructor before its body runs}, so a second constructor cannot forget it.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 Private} is enforced: it means {\i the class}, not the family. A subclass cannot see it, and neither can another class in the same file. What still works \u8212? and every generated form depends on it \u8212? is {\f1\fs18 AddressOf} on a private method of the same class:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 button.addActionListener(AddressOf OnClick)   ' OnClick may be Private\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b A form lives entirely in its instance.} Both designers hold to that rule, and it is why two sessions showing the same form are independent without anybody arranging it.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Shared fields\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Public Class Tools\line 
    Public Shared Counter As Integer = 0\line 
\line 
    Public Static Sub Report(text As String)     ' the same word, spelled differently\line 
        Counter += 1\line 
        Print "[" + Counter + "] " + text\line 
    End Sub\line 
End Class\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 Shared} and {\f1\fs18 Static} are two spellings of one idea \u8212? {\f1\fs18 Shared} from VB.NET, {\f1\fs18 Static} from Java, Xojo and B4J. In a {\f1\fs18 Module} neither is needed: everything there is shared anyway.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj One value {\b for the whole process}. On a desktop that means one value for the one user sitting there. On a server it means one value for everybody at once, and that is where the trouble starts.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Initialisers of shared fields run in the class initialiser, the first time the class is touched.\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 VB6's {\f1\fs18 Static} \u8212? a {\i local} that keeps its value between calls \u8212? is a different thing and is not built. It would stand inside a body, not in front of a declaration, so the two cannot collide.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Session statics\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Module Main\line 
    SessionStatic UserName As String\line 
    SessionStatic Basket As java.util.ArrayList\line 
\line 
    Sub Main()\line 
        ...\line 
    End Sub\line 
End Module\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Reachable exactly like a {\f1\fs18 Shared} \u8212? by bare name inside the module, qualified as {\f1\fs18 Main.UserName} from anywhere else \u8212? but {\b one value per session}. {\f1\fs18 SessionShared} is a synonym, for the same reason {\f1\fs18 Static} is a synonym for {\f1\fs18 Shared}.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj On a desktop a program has exactly one session, so a session static there behaves like an ordinary static. On a web server each user gets their own. {\b The same program, unchanged, isolates per user the moment a server is underneath it.} That is the whole point of the keyword, and Chapter 20 is about how it is built and when to use it.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Two rules the compiler enforces:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Public Class Holder\line 
    Public SessionStatic Counter As Integer     ' RFX0208\line 
End Class\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 error RFX0208: 'Counter' is SessionStatic, but 'Holder' does not hold 'Sub Main'.\line 
Session statics are declared in the main module only, so that every one of them is\line 
in one place.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Public Shared SessionStatic Counter As Integer  ' RFX0209\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 error RFX0209: 'Counter' cannot be both SessionStatic and Shared. A SessionStatic\line 
has one value per session; a Shared has one for all of them.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The session's own named values\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Beside session statics there is a second per-session store, reached through the session object itself:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 tsbWebSession.setValue("screen", "customers")\line 
Var screen As Object = tsbWebSession.getValue("screen")\line 
\line 
Print tsbWebSession.hasValue("screen")\line 
tsbWebSession.removeValue("screen")\line 
Var names As java.util.Set = tsbWebSession.valueNames()\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b This is the escape hatch, not the main road.} A declared {\f1\fs18 SessionStatic} is typed, is found by completion, and fails at compile time when it is misspelled. A name in this map is none of those things \u8212? {\f1\fs18 getValue("scren")} returns {\f1\fs18 Nothing} and says nothing. Use it when the set of keys is genuinely dynamic; use a {\f1\fs18 SessionStatic} otherwise.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The two are separate maps on purpose, even though merging them would save a field. The keys a session static uses are written by the compiler \u8212? {\f1\fs18 Main.Counter} \u8212? and a program that listed {\f1\fs18 valueNames()} would suddenly see them. Two maps, two audiences: one the program's own, one the language's.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The bare name {\f1\fs18 tsbWebSession}\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The compiler adds exactly one name for web programs, and it adds it {\b at the very end of name resolution}:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 If tsbWebSession.SignedIn Then\line 
    Print "Hello, " + CType(tsbWebSession.User.get(), tsbWebUser).DisplayName\line 
End If\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The name binds to a lookup of the current thread's session, and everything after that is an ordinary instance access. Static entry point, per-thread state \u8212? the same pattern {\f1\fs18 Err} already uses.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b A program's own member of that name always wins.} This was checked for every kind of thing a name can already be, because the lookup tries them in order and one of them being wrong would be enough:\par
