\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj So the addition cannot change the meaning of an existing program. It is also deliberately unlike {\f1\fs18 Err}, whose alias sits {\i before} the program's own types and therefore hides a {\f1\fs18 Class Err} of your own; that pattern was not copied.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The lookup itself is an {\b {\f1\fs18\b InheritableThreadLocal}}, so a background thread that your code starts inherits the session of the thread that started it. Without that, {\f1\fs18 tsbWebSession} would read {\f1\fs18 Nothing} inside every worker.\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 {\b A caveat, stated plainly.} The documentation for tsbWEB describes declaring your own {\f1\fs18 tsbWebSession} subclass with typed fields, and the compiler does support binding to it. The server, however, always creates the base {\f1\fs18 tsbWebSession}: {\f1\fs18 tsbWebSessionRegistry} has a {\f1\fs18 Factory} for a custom type, but {\f1\fs18 tsbWebServer} hard-codes the base one and {\f1\fs18 tsbWebConfig} has no setting for it. There is therefore {\b no way to get your own session class instantiated through {\f1\fs18\b tsbWebStart.Go}} as things stand. Until that hook exists, the typed, checked, completion-friendly route is {\f1\fs18 SessionStatic}, and the untyped one is {\f1\fs18 getValue} / {\f1\fs18 setValue}.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Scope on the desktop, scope on the web\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The point of the design is that these are the same list. A desktop program has one session; a web program has one per user. Nothing in between changes:\par
