\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\cellx4790
\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\cellx9580
\pard\plain\intbl\sb20\sa20\f0\fs19\cf1\b0 {\f1\fs18 Counter = 5} (write)\cell
\pard\plain\intbl\sb20\sa20\f0\fs19\cf1\b0 {\f1\fs18 LDC "Main.Counter"} \u183? the value, boxed \u183? {\f1\fs18 INVOKESTATIC RfxSessionStatics.put}\cell
\row
\pard\sb0\sa140

\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj There is no {\f1\fs18 GETSTATIC} and no {\f1\fs18 PUTSTATIC}, because there is no slot to address. {\b The isolation is the construction, not the discipline.}\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The key is {\f1\fs18 Owner.Field} \u8212? {\f1\fs18 Main.Counter} \u8212? qualified by the owning type so that two modules may each have a {\f1\fs18 Counter} without one reading the other's. It never reaches a user's eyes; it only has to be stable and unique.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The read side has {\b one runtime method per shape} \u8212? {\f1\fs18 getInt}, {\f1\fs18 getLong}, {\f1\fs18 getDouble}, {\f1\fs18 getFloat}, {\f1\fs18 getShort}, {\f1\fs18 getByte}, {\f1\fs18 getChar}, {\f1\fs18 getBoolean}, {\f1\fs18 getObject} \u8212? rather than one returning {\f1\fs18 Object}. The alternative writes a checkcast plus an unboxing sequence into the generated code at every read, in eight variants, and a compiler backend is the worst place to keep eight nearly identical things correct.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj For an object type a {\f1\fs18 CHECKCAST} follows, because {\f1\fs18 getObject} hands back an {\f1\fs18 Object} and every reader downstream was bound against the field's declared type. For an {\b array} the cast uses the descriptor form ({\f1\fs18 [Ljava/lang/String;}) rather than the internal name \u8212? otherwise a session-static array would come back as an {\f1\fs18 Object} and fail verification at the first {\f1\fs18 ARRAYLENGTH}.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Where the value lives\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 com.rapidfx.runtime.RfxSessionStatics} holds the mechanism and knows nothing about sessions. It asks its host exactly one question:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 @FunctionalInterface\line 
public interface Scope \{\line 
    Map<String, Object> current();\line 
\}\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\i Which map belongs to whoever is being served on this thread.}\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b With nothing installed} there is {\b one {\f1\fs18\b ConcurrentHashMap} for the process}. That is not a placeholder; it is the correct answer when there is one user, and it is what makes a desktop program behave sensibly.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b tsbWEB installs a different scope at start-up}, which answers with the current session's own map.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Why the mechanism lives in the runtime and not in tsbWEB:} because it has to work on a desktop too, and that is the whole point of it. Had it lived in tsbWEB, every desktop project would have needed the server jars just to declare a variable \u8212? and the migration story would have been a lie.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The server installs it {\b reflectively}, and for the same reason the look and feel is installed reflectively next door: tsbWEB must not depend on the tsbRapidFX runtime. It is a Gradle build resolving from Maven Central; the runtime is a jar from a sibling checkout, packed inside the finished program's own jar.\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 static boolean install() \{\line 
    Class<?> statics = Class.forName("com.rapidfx.runtime.RfxSessionStatics");\line 
    Class<?> scope   = Class.forName("com.rapidfx.runtime.RfxSessionStatics$Scope");\line 
    Object handler = Proxy.newProxyInstance(..., (proxy, method, args) ->\line 
            "current".equals(method.getName()) ? mapForThisThread() : ...);\line 
    statics.getMethod("install", scope).invoke(null, handler);\line 
    return true;\line 
\}\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Three outcomes, all deliberate:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b The class is absent} \u8212? the program declares no session static and carries no tsbRapidFX runtime. Nothing to install, which is not an error, so it reports nothing and moves on. This is the normal case for a server written in Java.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b The class is there and takes the hook} \u8212? the usual case.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b The class is there and refuses the hook} \u8212? reported {\b loudly} on standard error, because session statics would then silently be shared between users.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 mapForThisThread()} asks the session scope. For a thread that serves no session \u8212? the server's own plumbing, or a test \u8212? it answers with a private map nobody else can see. Better than throwing: this is reached from generated code that has no idea it is being asked about sessions, and an exception out of a field read would surface a long way from the mistake.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj This is where it lands in the session:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 /** Where this session's SessionStatic variables keep their values. */\line 
private final Map<String, Object> sessionStatics = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Concurrent}, because a background thread the program started inherits this session and may touch a session static from there.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Untouched means empty\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Reading a value nobody has written gives {\b 0, False or Nothing} \u8212? the same as an uninitialised static in VB, and the same in every session.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj There is deliberately {\b no way to pre-fill one for everybody}. That would be a shared value again, wearing this keyword as a disguise. If you need a starting value per session, set it where the session begins:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Public Function createRoot(session As tsbWebSession) As Component\line 
    If Main.Basket Is Nothing Then Main.Basket = New java.util.ArrayList()\line 
    ...\line 
End Function\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Writing {\f1\fs18 Nothing} {\b removes} the entry rather than storing it, because a {\f1\fs18 ConcurrentHashMap} will not hold a null \u8212? and "set it back to Nothing" has to work, or the keyword is a trap.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The measurements\par
