\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A session static would have to be touched {\b about fourteen thousand times} to equal the cost of a single event reaching the browser. In an event handler \u8212? which is where a session static is read \u8212? the number is going to be single digits.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Where the extra nanoseconds go is no mystery: a {\f1\fs18 ThreadLocal.get}, a hash of a short constant string, and one {\f1\fs18 ConcurrentHashMap} lookup. The string is a compile-time constant in the class file's pool, so its hash is computed once and cached by {\f1\fs18 String} itself.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Memory.} One entry per declared session static per session, in a {\f1\fs18 ConcurrentHashMap}: on the order of 50 bytes plus the value. Measured against 209 kB of heap per session at 3,800 sessions (Chapter 21), a dozen session statics per user are not detectable.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 When Static, when SessionStatic\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj This is the rule, and it is short.\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 {\b {\f1\fs18\b Shared} is for what every user may see alike. {\f1\fs18\b SessionStatic} is for what belongs to one user.}\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 Shared} \u8212? one value for the whole process \u8212? is right for:\par
\trowd\trgaph80\trleft0\trpaddl80\trpaddr80\trpaddt40\trpaddb40
