\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The test to apply at the declaration, in one sentence: {\b would it be a bug if the next person to sign in saw this value?} If yes, {\f1\fs18 SessionStatic}. If no, {\f1\fs18 Shared}.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj And the corollary that catches the remaining cases: {\b if you are unsure, take {\f1\fs18\b SessionStatic}.} Being wrong that way costs seven nanoseconds. Being wrong the other way costs a data breach.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Two more rules the compiler enforces, and why\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b All session statics are declared in the main module} ({\f1\fs18 RFX0208}). They are reachable from everywhere, so they belong where everybody can find them. Scattered across twenty classes they would be exactly what statics always become \u8212? and the point of the keyword is that the reader of a program can see, on one screen, everything that is per-user in it.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The web project template makes that visible by writing the two headings out:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Module Main\line 
\line 
'******************************************************************************\line 
'                              SESSION STATICS\line 
'\line 
'   Sessionstatic variables are variables reachable from everywhere inside of\line 
'   a Session. Every user gets their own value; nobody can read anybody else's.\line 
'\line 
'   Declared here and nowhere else, so that all of them are on one screen.\line 
'******************************************************************************\line 
\line 
    ' SessionStatic UserName As String\line 
\line 
\line 
'******************************************************************************\line 
'                              STATIC VARIABLES\line 
'\line 
'   This static variables are reachable from everywhere in every session.\line 
'\line 
'   Only for what every user may see alike - a catalogue, a price list, a\line 
'   configuration. A user's own data in one of these is a data leak: the second\line 
'   person to sign in would read the first one's.\line 
'******************************************************************************\line 
\line 
    ' Shared PriceListVersion As String\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b A field cannot be both} ({\f1\fs18 RFX0209}). One holds per session, the other for everyone. Guessing which was meant would be the wrong kindness.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Migrating a desktop program to the web\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj This is what the whole construction buys, so it is worth stating as a procedure:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 1.\tab Find every {\f1\fs18 Shared} field in the program.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 2.\tab For each, ask the question above: would it be a bug if the next user saw this?\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 3.\tab Change the ones that answer yes to {\f1\fs18 SessionStatic}, and move them into the main module.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 4.\tab Change nothing else.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The program still runs identically on the desktop, because there a session static {\i is} a static \u8212? one session, one map. And it is now correct on a server, because the isolation is in the emitted code rather than in anybody's memory.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj That is the whole migration for state. What remains is windows becoming screens (Chapter 15) and the entry point becoming a server (Chapter 18).\par
\pard\sb160\sa160\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb400\sa140\f2\b\fs34\cf2 21 The server: what it carries, and how\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Everything in this chapter is measured. Where a number has no measurement behind it, it is not here.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The architecture, in one picture\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Browser                          |  Server (one JVM)\line 
                                 |\line 
per node: painted SVG            |  tsbWebSessionRegistry\line 
  and on top a real <input>      |    +- tsbWebSession   (per user, no static state)\line 
  wherever typing or choosing    |    +- session thread  (platform, serial)\line 
  ^ patch (SSE)                  |    +- component registry (int <-> Component)\line 
  v event (POST)                 |\line 
                                 |  tsbWebMirror         - tree to JSON, patch computation\line 
                                 |  tsbWebGraphics2D     - the look and feel paints to SVG\line 
                                 |  tsbWebRepaintManager - what needs repainting\line 
                                 |  real javax.swing underneath\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The insight it all rests on\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Swing computes the geometry for us.} Layout managers, font metrics and look-and-feel sizes all run without a screen. The browser gets finished pixel coordinates and positions absolutely; {\b no layout is rebuilt in CSS or JavaScript}. That is why "replace Swing components with JS components" works here at all: the hard half stays in Java.\par
