\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj From which the cut follows: {\b only top-level windows are replaced. Everything below is real Swing.}\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The session thread\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b One platform thread per session, not a virtual one.} AWT takes the {\f1\fs18 synchronized} monitor {\f1\fs18 Component$AWTTreeLock} on its way through layout, and {\f1\fs18 synchronized} pins a virtual thread to its carrier on Java 21. For the SSE writers, virtual threads {\i are} right \u8212? both variants hold 200 streams.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Incoming events go through a {\f1\fs18 BlockingQueue} into exactly that thread. So there is {\b no concurrency within a session and no shared tree between sessions}. Measured: 200 sessions \u215? 100 events, 0 errors, identical geometry in every thread.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj That single decision is what makes the rest of the model honest \u8212? and it is also the thing that sets the ceiling, as the numbers below show.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Mirroring\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 tsbWebMirror} walks the component tree and produces one node each:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 "42": \{ "t": "JButton", "x": 16, "y": 124, "w": 388, "h": 46,\line 
        "text": "+1", "fn": "SansSerif,bold,18",\line 
        "fg": "#000000", "bg": "#eeeeee", "kids": [ 43, 44 ] \}\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The id is the key, not a field: the whole tree travels as {\f1\fs18 \{ "kind": "tree", "root": 1, "theme": \{ ... \}, "nodes": \{ "42": \{ ... \} \} \}}. {\f1\fs18 kids} holds ids, not nested nodes \u8212? the tree is flat on the wire and put together again in the browser.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 vis} and {\f1\fs18 on} appear {\b only when false}. Everything visible and enabled says so by saying nothing, which is the common case and therefore the one that should cost no bytes.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Measured: 30 nodes is about 1,788 bytes as a full tree. After the first build, only {\b patches} go over the wire \u8212? lists of changed fields per id, plus structural changes on add and remove.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Ids are per-session consecutive integers}, held in an {\f1\fs18 IdentityHashMap<Component,Integer>} and a reverse map. Class names, hash codes and field names {\b never} cross the wire. An incoming event names only a number, resolved against the registry of {\i that} session; a foreign or invented id finds nothing.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Painting, and why it is fast\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 tsbWebRepaintManager} is the reason the numbers below are what they are. Painting is the expensive half of a patch, and usually exactly one component has changed. Swing already knows this \u8212? every {\f1\fs18 repaint()}, {\f1\fs18 setText} and {\f1\fs18 setEnabled} lands at the {\f1\fs18 RepaintManager} \u8212? so here it collects those reports for the mirror instead of scheduling a paint.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The mirror repaints a component when {\b either} a property it reads anyway has changed (bounds, text, selection, enabled) {\b or} Swing reported it dirty. The second catches everything we do not know about \u8212? a custom {\f1\fs18 paintComponent} reading a model of its own. A component that is neither changed nor reported keeps its picture.\par
