\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A factor of 64, and almost all of it was one image being re-encoded as PNG on every patch.\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 {\f1\fs18 RepaintManager.setCurrentManager} is {\b the only process-wide thing in the whole design}, because that is how the class is built. What it holds is component references and nothing else \u8212? no user data. A component belongs to exactly one session for its whole life, so an entry can only be collected by the session it belongs to.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The look and feel paints itself, as SVG\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 tsbWebGraphics2D} is a {\f1\fs18 Graphics2D} that does not rasterise the drawing commands but records them as SVG. Every component is therefore drawn by {\b its own} look and feel \u8212? FlatLaf's tick, its dropdown arrow, its slider knob, the title of a {\f1\fs18 TitledBorder}, and anything a program paints itself. No CSS re-implementation.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Why that stays small.} Measured against FlatLaf, drawing ten components calls {\f1\fs18 fill(Shape)}, {\f1\fs18 drawString}, {\f1\fs18 setColor}, {\f1\fs18 setFont}, {\f1\fs18 clipRect}, {\f1\fs18 translate} and {\f1\fs18 create} \u8212? and almost nothing else. {\f1\fs18 draw(Shape)} does not appear at all: FlatLaf paints a border as a filled {\f1\fs18 Area} (outside minus inside). Two things fold the rest together:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab A {\f1\fs18 PathIterator} reduces {\i every} shape \u8212? {\f1\fs18 Area}, {\f1\fs18 RoundRectangle2D}, {\f1\fs18 Ellipse2D}, {\f1\fs18 Path2D} \u8212? to the same four segment kinds. One loop, no special cases.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\f1\fs18 Stroke.createStrokedShape} turns a stroke into a fillable shape, so {\f1\fs18 draw} is a {\f1\fs18 fill} of a different shape. Strokes, caps and joins come out exactly as Java2D would have computed them \u8212? because Java2D did compute them.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Text stays text.} As {\f1\fs18 <text>}, not as outlines: outlines would not be selectable, would be unreadable to a screen reader, and would be several times the size. Instead Java measures the string itself and writes the width as {\f1\fs18 textLength}, so the browser stretches its own glyphs to exactly the width Swing measured.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Who is painted how:\par
