\pard\sb0\sa140

\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Text fields get only the border because caret, selection and input method cannot be painted. Containers do not paint their children \u8212? those are separate nodes, or the screen would be on the page twice and a changed label would mean re-sending the whole form.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b The three mouse states travel in advance.} A button arrives as {\f1\fs18 svg}, {\f1\fs18 svgH} (hover) and {\f1\fs18 svgP} (pressed), so the browser can follow the pointer without a round trip. Two sharp traps live there, both handled: {\f1\fs18 setPressed(false)} fires the action on an {\i armed} button \u8212? hence {\f1\fs18 setArmed(false)} first, or the click handler would run on every patch \u8212? and {\f1\fs18 setRollover} fires {\f1\fs18 ChangeListener}s, which are detached beforehand and reattached in a {\f1\fs18 finally}.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The colour rule\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Five things the look and feel does {\b not} paint: the text {\i inside} an {\f1\fs18 input}, the rows of a list, table or tree, the menu titles, the opened menu panel, and the frame of a dialog. Whatever is not stated for those is not "already known" but unknown, and the browser takes its own black \u8212? the page declares no {\f1\fs18 color-scheme}, so the light form of {\f1\fs18 CanvasText} applies even on a dark desktop.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj In a light theme this is invisible. In a dark one it is black text in a dark field.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The carrier is {\f1\fs18 tsbWebTheme}: it reads the {\f1\fs18 UIDefaults}, travels as CSS variables in the tree message, and is applied through the CSSOM (a {\f1\fs18 <style>} block would have failed the {\f1\fs18 style-src 'self'} content security policy). Guarded by a test of {\b 50 themes \u215? 20 probes}, comparing desktop colour against browser colour. Before the rule was found, {\b 878 of 1,000 comparisons disagreed.}\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 The practical form of the rule: a literal colour written into {\f1\fs18 app.css} is very probably a bug. The only exceptions are the two things that belong to the page itself \u8212? the veil over a dialog, and the connection-lost line.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Security\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Session id:} 256 bits from {\f1\fs18 new SecureRandom()}, cookie {\f1\fs18 HttpOnly; SameSite=Strict; Path=/}, plus {\f1\fs18 Secure} on an HTTPS server. {\b Never in the URL} \u8212? it would land in server logs and in the {\f1\fs18 Referer}. Explicitly {\b not} {\f1\fs18 SecureRandom.getInstanceStrong()}: that is {\f1\fs18 NativePRNGBlocking} here and can hang on low entropy, and a server that blocks while creating a session is an outage. For session ids the non-blocking provider is cryptographically sufficient.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Session fixation:} after a successful sign-in a new id is issued and the old one discarded, together with the CSRF token. The state of the unauthenticated visit is deliberately discarded, not carried over.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b How the id rotates without an HTTP response.} Sign-in happens inside a Swing handler, where there is no response left for a {\f1\fs18 Set-Cookie}. So the new id goes out over the event stream, and the browser sends it thereafter in the header {\f1\fs18 X-tsbWeb-Session}, which the server checks {\b before} the cookie. That is not merely a way round the problem, it is the better arrangement: an id that lives only in the browser's memory cannot be planted from outside, and session fixation has nothing left to plant.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b CSRF:} every {\f1\fs18 POST} must carry {\f1\fs18 X-tsbWeb-Csrf}, compared in constant time. The cookie alone is no proof, because the browser sends it on foreign-triggered requests too.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b The client can name no class and no method} \u8212? only component ids from its own session.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Limits:} message at most 64 kB, file at most 20 MB; event rate limited per session; idle timeout 30 minutes by default.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b No default ceiling on the number of sessions.} A session is created for every visitor, signed in or not, so a crawler would fill any number small enough to protect memory \u8212? and then no real user gets in. The limit would be the attack, not the defence. What protects is the idle timeout. Whoever wants a ceiling anyway sets {\f1\fs18 sitzungen.max}.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Passwords are checked with {\f1\fs18 PBKDF2WithHmacSHA256}, 210,000 rounds, salted per user \u8212? 83 ms per attempt, contained in the JDK, and it saves the dependency bcrypt or Argon2 would cost. Nothing is stored in clear and nothing is logged.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Capacity: the numbers\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Two campaigns, both with server and load generator in {\b separate JVMs} \u8212? otherwise the test client's own sockets count against the server.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Throughput, 1,000 sessions, 10 cores:}\par
