\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Read that table twice, because it is not the shape people expect.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b At 3,000 concurrent sessions the server is not merely surviving; it is at its best.} A page builds in 19 ms at the median and 58 ms at the 99th percentile \u8212? {\i better} than at 500 sessions, where p99 was 229 ms. The improvement between 500 and 2,000 is warm-up: by a thousand sessions the JIT has compiled every path that matters and the heap has settled, and the per-session work is genuinely independent, so more sessions mostly means better-used cores.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The curve stays flat to 3,500 and only lifts at 3,800, close under the ceiling. At 3,800 simultaneous sessions: {\b 3,833 threads, 3,801 open connections, 794 MB of heap \u8212? about 209 kB per session \u8212? and a {\i new} visitor is served the page in under 1.5 ms.}\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Where the hard wall is\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Not memory. The operating system's thread limit.}\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj tsbWEB holds one platform thread per session, and macOS allows a process {\f1\fs18 kern.num_taskthreads} = {\b 4096}. Above that the process dies, and unambiguously:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 pthread_create failed (EAGAIN)\line 
java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: unable to create native thread\line 
    at com.tsbweb.server.tsbWebLiveSession.start\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj That is not a heap problem \u8212? with {\f1\fs18 -Xmx6g} less than a sixth was in use. {\b On Linux {\f1\fs18\b /proc/sys/kernel/threads-max} is orders of magnitude higher}, so the same application goes considerably further there. It is worth checking anyway: inside a container the cgroup's {\f1\fs18 pids.max} limits it as well.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Practically: {\b on a Mac do not plan above about 3,500}; on Linux look up the machine's own limits; beyond that, a load balancer and several processes.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The second wall, met before the threads, is the process-wide {\f1\fs18 Component$AWTTreeLock}. Measured: {\b 15,700 layout operations per second} against a realistic 200\u8211?400. The p99 of 13.5 ms already includes that contention under forty times realistic load. Both walls lie far beyond the recommended figure.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The recommended figure, and why it is lower\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Up to 750 concurrent users per server}, depending on the size and load of the application.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The number is deliberately far below what was measured, and the reason is not caution about tsbWEB:\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 {\b The measuring screen does nothing.} It paints and computes layout. A real application queries a database, and a handler that waits 200 ms on SQL blocks its session thread \u8212? exactly as on the desktop. What a server really carries depends on the handlers, not on tsbWEB.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj 750 leaves room for that, and for the morning when everybody arrives at once. Memory is nowhere the limit: at 1.2 MB per session a 64 GB machine would carry tens of thousands on paper.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Above that, put a load balancer in front. {\b Sessions are not shared between JVMs}: each user must stay with their server (sticky sessions).\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Operating it\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 java -Xmx4g -jar CustomerManagement.jar\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b No {\f1\fs18\b -Djava.awt.headless=true} needed} \u8212? {\f1\fs18 tsbWebStart} sets it first of all, before anything touches AWT. Whoever calls the server without {\f1\fs18 tsbWebStart} must pass it on the command line: the server never draws to a screen, and without the flag AWT looks for a display and fails on a machine without one.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b No X server, no Xvfb, no display} is required. That is the difference from tools that render into an image server-side.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 What is missing, named honestly\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj There is {\b no operations console, no metrics endpoint, no cluster and no session failover}. A restart ends every session. For the intended scale that is acceptable; whoever needs more should know it beforehand.\par
\pard\plain\page\keepn\sb0\sa280\qc\f2\b\fs52\cf2 Part VI \u8212? The designers\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb400\sa140\f2\b\fs34\cf2 22 The desktop designer\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b tsbDesignerSWX} is a visual form designer for Java Swing that generates tsbRapidFX. No XML, no build tool, no intermediate file format.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 One form, one file, one banner\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Every form is exactly {\b one {\f1\fs18\b .rfx} file}, divided by a banner:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Imports javax.swing\line 
Imports java.awt\line 
Imports java.awt.event\line 
\line 
Public Class LoginForm\line 
\line 
    Public Sub New()\line 
        InitDesignerComponents()\line 
    End Sub\line 
\line 
    ' Handles OnBtnLoginClick - the body is yours.\line 
    Private Sub OnBtnLoginClick(e As ActionEvent)\line 
        Print "that was me"\line 
    End Sub\line 
\line 
\line 
\line 
'*****************************************************************************\line 
'*****************************************************************************\line 
'*****************************************************************************\line 
'\line 
'                                 DO NOT EDIT\line 
'                             TSB RAPIDFX LOGIC\line 
'\line 
'*****************************************************************************\line 
'*****************************************************************************\line 
'*****************************************************************************\line 
\line 
\line 
\line 
    ' @tsbswx form class=LoginForm root=AnchorPanel platform=DESKTOP w=1280 h=800 showIn=true\line 
    Private rootPane As JPanel = New JPanel()\line 
    Private rootPaneLayout As SpringLayout = New SpringLayout()\line 
\line 
    ' @tsbswx Button btnLogin parent=rootPane L=24 T=100 W=120 H=30\line 
    Private btnLogin As JButton = New JButton()\line 
\line 
    Private Sub InitDesignerComponents()\line 
        rootPane.setName("rootPane")\line 
        rootPane.setLayout(rootPaneLayout)\line 
        rootPane.setPreferredSize(New Dimension(1280, 800))\line 
\line 
        btnLogin.setName("btnLogin")\line 
        btnLogin.setText("Sign in")\line 
        btnLogin.addActionListener(AddressOf OnBtnLoginClick)\line 
        btnLogin.setPreferredSize(New Dimension(120, 30))\line 
        rootPaneLayout.putConstraint(SpringLayout.WEST, btnLogin, 24, SpringLayout.WEST, rootPane)\line 
        rootPaneLayout.putConstraint(SpringLayout.NORTH, btnLogin, 100, SpringLayout.NORTH, rootPane)\line 
        rootPane.add(btnLogin)\line 
    End Sub\line 
\line 
    Public Function GetRootPane() As JPanel\line 
        Return rootPane\line 
    End Function\line 
\line 
    Public Sub ShowIn(frame As JFrame)\line 
        frame.setTitle("LoginForm")\line 
        frame.setContentPane(rootPane)\line 
        frame.setSize(1280, 800)\line 
        frame.setLocationRelativeTo(Nothing)\line 
        frame.setVisible(True)\line 
    End Sub\line 
\line 
End Class\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj (The rules are drawn seventy-eight characters wide; they are shortened here to fit the page.)\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Above the banner is yours}: the constructor, event handlers, helper methods, more fields. The designer writes that half back character for character and only ever {\i adds} two things \u8212? a missing {\f1\fs18 Imports} and an empty body for an event you wired up. Both are purely additive; nothing is ever removed.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Below the banner} are the component fields and the whole build method. That half is rewritten in full on every save.\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 {\b Why the banner is large.} It replaced a single comment line. One line reads like every other comment in the file, and the one thing a reader must not do here is edit below it \u8212? so the warning was given the weight of the consequence: three rules of asterisks, four blank lines of air on each side, and the words spelled out. It costs twenty lines once per file and buys a boundary nobody scrolls past. It also says {\b which} designer wrote the file, desktop or web. The older one-line marker is still recognised and never written again. A file carrying it opens, and the first save replaces it with the banner \u8212? so old forms upgrade themselves rather than becoming unopenable.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 AddressOf is what makes event wiring possible\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 btnLogin.addActionListener(AddressOf OnBtnLoginClick)\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 AddressOf} on a {\b private instance method} carries {\f1\fs18 Me} with it, so the handler can touch every field of the form while its body stays entirely yours. That one language feature is the reason the designer can generate event wiring at all without owning the behaviour.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 What the meta line is for\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The {\f1\fs18 ' @tsbswx} lines are tsbRapidFX comments \u8212? the compiler ignores them entirely. They exist so the designer can {\i reopen} the file.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Most of a form can be read back out of the code: {\f1\fs18 btnLogin.setText("Sign in")} says exactly what the caption is. Some things cannot be:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Whether 24 pixels from the left is an anchor or a frozen position.} The code has {\f1\fs18 putConstraint(WEST, btnLogin, 24, WEST, rootPane)} either way.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Settings a layout manager swallows.} A {\f1\fs18 FlowPanel} writes {\f1\fs18 New FlowLayout(FlowLayout.LEFT, 12, 12)} and you could read that back \u8212? but a {\f1\fs18 BoxLayout} has no spacing at all, and a {\f1\fs18 GridLayout} forgets its column count, so the number would simply be gone next time.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj So: what the code states unambiguously is read from the code; what it cannot state travels in the meta line. {\f1\fs18 L=24 T=100} are the anchors, {\f1\fs18 W=120 H=30} the size, {\f1\fs18 p.hgap=12} a design-time setting. {\b Delete the line and you lose the layout on the next open \u8212? not the program.} It still compiles and runs.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 No statics\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A form lives entirely in its instance:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 New LoginForm().ShowIn(frame)\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Two instances of the same form are independent. There is not one {\f1\fs18 Shared} field in the generated code, and none in the designer either: catalogue, undo stack, renderer and open forms all hang off a context the window owns. That is also what makes a form usable on a web server without a single change (Chapter 18).\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Only deviations are saved\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A property still at its declared default produces {\b no line}. A value that is set back to the default is forgotten again, which keeps the generated half small and readable \u8212? it says exactly what you changed.\par
