\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Six of the containers are the same {\f1\fs18 JPanel} with a different layout manager. That is not a shortcut; it is what Swing is. Inventing a {\f1\fs18 VBoxPanel} class would put a type into the generated code that the JDK does not have.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Why SpringLayout\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b An anchor is not a position \u8212? it is a rule about what happens when the window resizes.} The obvious translation of an anchor pane, a null layout with {\f1\fs18 setBounds}, throws that away and freezes the design into pixels.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 SpringLayout} is the one Swing manager that can state it: {\i this component's west edge is 24 from the parent's west edge}. Set both anchors on an axis and the component stretches, exactly as the canvas shows.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Each anchor container therefore gets a {\f1\fs18 SpringLayout} field of its own, because the constraints live on the layout rather than on the child. And {\b a right or bottom anchor is a negative distance} \u8212? a constraint always reads "this edge is N from that edge", so sitting 24 in from the right is -24 from {\f1\fs18 EAST}.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Each {\f1\fs18 GridBagConstraints} is built inline rather than as a shared, mutated local. A reused {\f1\fs18 GridBagConstraints} is the classic Swing bug \u8212? every later child silently inherits the previous one's span \u8212? and a form the designer wrote must not contain it.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The layout engine is {\b solver-free}: every rule is a direct formula, so the preview and the generated code cannot drift.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The preview is not a drawing\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The canvas paints {\b real Swing components}. A configured {\f1\fs18 JButton} is built and painted through a {\f1\fs18 CellRendererPane} \u8212? the mechanism {\f1\fs18 JTable} uses for every cell it draws \u8212? without the component ever belonging to a container.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj This is the single biggest difference from the JavaFX designer, which had to hand-draw an approximation of all seventy controls, because a Swing application cannot instantiate a JavaFX button. A Swing designer has no such problem. What follows:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab the preview is {\b pixel-identical} to the running form, and cannot drift from it;\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab a new component type needs no painter \u8212? 1,158 lines of hand-drawn painters are simply gone;\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab every FlatLaf theme is honoured exactly, because FlatLaf is what draws it.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj One prototype per type is reconfigured on each paint, restoring the {\i captured} original foreground, background, font and border between paints \u8212? rather than setting null, which is the obvious thing and is wrong: null on a Swing component means null, and the first component to paint with a null font takes the whole canvas down.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Themes\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b View \u9656? Themes} lists the four core FlatLaf themes plus the roughly forty ported IntelliJ themes, discovered from the jar rather than typed out.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Selecting a theme is the preview.} Moving through the list repaints the whole window, canvas included; Cancel puts back the one that was there.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj That is simpler than its JavaFX ancestor, which needed two steps \u8212? apply, then copy the stylesheet into your project \u8212? and the reason is worth saying. A JavaFX theme is a CSS file to be found, parsed and planted. A FlatLaf theme is a class in a jar the project already declares. Nothing to copy, and nothing to approximate.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The trade is that {\b a Swing look and feel is global}. The theme is remembered per form and travels in the meta line, but switching tabs changes the whole window rather than one canvas.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Installing the look and feel in the built program is one line in your {\f1\fs18 Sub Main}, not in the form:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Imports com.formdev.flatlaf\line 
FlatDarkLaf.setup()\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 What stays design time\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Four things the designer deliberately does not generate:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Chart data.} Series come from your application at run time. A generated {\f1\fs18 addSeries} you would have to delete first is worse than an empty chart.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b List and table contents.} A {\f1\fs18 JList} has no {\f1\fs18 addItem}; its content is a model the application owns.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Spinner ranges.} A {\f1\fs18 SpinnerNumberModel} is a line for your half of the file.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Container spacing, gaps and column counts.} A {\f1\fs18 BoxLayout} has no spacing and a {\f1\fs18 GridLayout} forgets its column count \u8212? so these travel in the meta line, which is exactly what it is for.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Operating it\par
