\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Dragging over a container marks it with a green dashed outline \u8212? that is where the component lands.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The check that matters\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 ./gradlew verifyGeneratedCode\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj It generates one form per component type \u8212? with {\b every} property moved off its default \u8212? and runs the real {\f1\fs18 rfxc} over them. It needs the compiler built next door.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A round-trip test only proves the designer can read back what it wrote. {\b Only a compiler proves that what it wrote is valid tsbRapidFX.} On its first run it found four calls that looked entirely reasonable and do not exist:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\f1\fs18 setMinWidth} / {\f1\fs18 setMinHeight} / {\f1\fs18 setMaxWidth} / {\f1\fs18 setMaxHeight} \u8212? carried over from JavaFX, where a region really has them. A Swing component has {\f1\fs18 setMinimumSize(Dimension)}.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\f1\fs18 XChartPanel.setTitle} \u8212? the title belongs to the chart inside the panel, not to the panel.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\f1\fs18 getChart().setXAxisTitle} \u8212? that one is on a subclass of the type the getter returns, and tsbRapidFX erases type arguments, so the call is made against the base type and does not compile.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\f1\fs18 setMnemonic("S"c)} \u8212? tsbRapidFX has no character literal. It is {\f1\fs18 "S".charAt(0)}.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Every one of those passed the whole unit suite first.} The task is not optional; run it after any change to the catalogue.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Known limits\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Renaming a component} changes the generated field name. If your code above the banner uses it you get a compile error \u8212? deliberately visible, but with no automatic refactoring.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Unused imports stay} when a component is deleted. An import you added by hand matters more than a tidy list, so the designer only ever adds.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b There is no package field.} tsbRapidFX has no packages, so there was nothing for it to say.\par
\pard\sb160\sa160\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb400\sa140\f2\b\fs34\cf2 23 The web designer\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj There is {\b one designer and two targets}. The web designer is not a second program: it is the same designer, told that this form is drawn for a browser.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj That is the point. A form is ordinary Swing either way \u8212? the same {\f1\fs18 JPanel} with the same layout managers \u8212? so the difference comes down to three things.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The three differences, and nothing else\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b 1. No {\f1\fs18\b ShowIn(JFrame)} is generated.}\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj On a desktop, {\f1\fs18 ShowIn} is what a form is normally used through. On a server there is no frame: the browser is the window, and {\f1\fs18 new JFrame()} throws {\f1\fs18 HeadlessException} in the {\f1\fs18 Window} constructor. A generated {\f1\fs18 ShowIn} would be a method nobody can call, in every file.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj So a web form ends at:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1     Public Function GetRootPane() As JPanel\line 
        Return rootPane\line 
    End Function\line 
\line 
End Class\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj and that is exactly the handle {\f1\fs18 createRoot} needs:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 Public Function createRoot(session As tsbWebSession) As Component\line 
    Return New MainForm().GetRootPane()\line 
End Function\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj It stays {\b switchable rather than removed}, because a form drawn for a browser is ordinary Swing and may well be wanted on a desktop too. The meta line records the choice as {\f1\fs18 showIn=true} or {\f1\fs18 showIn=false}.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b 2. The design surface means something different.}\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj On the desktop it is a window. On the web it is a {\b browser viewport} \u8212? which is why the phone and tablet sizes matter there and not here.\par
