\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The orientation is {\b derived from the dimensions}, not stored beside them \u8212? the other way round was a bug in the designer this one takes its metric from, where a hard-coded portrait orientation rotated every landscape platform. The toolbar's rotate button simply swaps width and height.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The size is written into the code as well as into the meta line:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 rootPane.setPreferredSize(New Dimension(1280, 800))\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj That line matters more on the web than on the desktop. A meta line is read by the designer and by nobody else; the code is what runs, and the server lays the tree out headless from its preferred size. {\b A form that lost that line came out too short in the browser} \u8212? which is exactly what happened once, and is the reason it is generated.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b 3. The banner says which one it is.}\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 '                                 DO NOT EDIT\line 
'                             TSB RAPIDFX WEB LOGIC\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj against the desktop's {\f1\fs18 TSB RAPIDFX LOGIC}. So the difference that matters most about a form is legible before reading a line of its code.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Where the target is recorded\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The target travels in the {\f1\fs18 ' @tsbswx form} line, so {\b a file carries its own answer} and nothing has to be guessed from the folder it sits in:\par
\pard\plain\li260\ri200\sb120\sa160\sl240\slmult1\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf4\brsp40\cbpat3\f1\fs17\cf1 ' @tsbswx form class=MainForm root=AnchorPanel platform=DESKTOP w=1280 h=800 showIn=false target=WEB\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\cbpat0\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The project says the same thing in its {\f1\fs18 .rfxproj}. When the IDE starts the designer it passes that on, so a {\b new} form is born with the right target already set.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b A project is desktop {\i or} web}, fixed when it is created. There is no run-time switch; that would be an application having to serve two programming models at once.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Opening it from the IDE\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Right-click a {\f1\fs18 .rfx} file \u8594? {\b Open in Designer}. The IDE hands over three things it already knows, so the designer does not have to ask:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 1.\tab the file,\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 2.\tab the source folder to save into,\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 3.\tab whether this is a web project or a desktop one \u8212? from the {\f1\fs18 .rfxproj}.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Everything unsaved goes to disk first. The designer reads the file, not the editor's buffer, and opening it on a version the user cannot see is the kind of confusion that ends with somebody losing work. After the designer saves, the file is reloaded on focus.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Why a separate process and not an editor tab\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\f1\fs18 UIManager} is JVM-wide. A designer embedded in the IDE therefore cannot install FlatLaf without repainting the IDE, so an embedded canvas draws with the {\b IDE's} look and feel.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj On the desktop that would be ugly. {\b In a web project it would be wrong}, and that is the sharper reason: the server paints every component into SVG {\i with FlatLaf's own delegates} (Chapter 21), so a canvas drawn by anything else is showing something the browser will never show. The preview would be lying.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj In its own JVM the designer installs FlatLaf properly. The canvas is then the same drawing the browser gets, the theme can be switched, and it runs at the speed of the standalone application \u8212? because it {\i is} the standalone application.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The designer is carried inside the plugin as a resource jar and unpacked into the IDE's system directory on first use.\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 {\b An operational note worth knowing.} That unpacking was once keyed only by file name, so the first unpack held forever: installing a freshly built plugin did not help and {\i could} not help, because the running designer came from a stale copy in the IDE cache. It is now keyed by the content's own SHA-256, written through a neighbouring file with {\f1\fs18 ATOMIC_MOVE}, with older unpackings deleted best-effort. If a designer ever behaves like an older version, look in the IDE cache directory before looking at the source.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The theme, and why it cannot drift\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The look and feel of a web application lives in {\f1\fs18 Sub Main} as {\f1\fs18 config.Thema} (Chapter 18). {\b Choose a theme in the designer, and saving the form updates that line} \u8212? so the preview and the server cannot disagree.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Verified by measurement rather than by eye: background {\f1\fs18 #ffffff} / {\f1\fs18 #000000} light against {\f1\fs18 #333333} / {\f1\fs18 #eeeeee} dark, compared between the designer's canvas and the browser's page.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 What a web form may contain\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Everything the desktop catalogue has. The components are the same Swing classes, and Chapter 21 lists how each is delivered \u8212? buttons painted in all three mouse states, text fields as a painted border with a real {\f1\fs18 <input>} inside, tables and trees as structure rather than as a picture.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Two consequences worth planning for while drawing:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b A dialog is not a window.} Draw the dialog's contents as an ordinary form and hand its {\f1\fs18 GetRootPane()} to a {\f1\fs18 tsbWebDialog} (Chapter 15). There is nothing to draw differently.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b A screen change is not a window change.} Draw each screen as its own form; {\f1\fs18 createRoot} picks between them.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Creating a web project\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b New Project \u8594? RapidFX \u8212? Web} writes three {\f1\fs18 .rfx} files, copies four jars into {\f1\fs18 lib/}, and adds the project file, {\f1\fs18 tsbweb.properties}, a README and a {\f1\fs18 .gitignore}. No Maven, no Gradle, no network.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Two of the three files are {\b built by the designer's own generator}, not pasted in as text resources. That is deliberate: a {\f1\fs18 .rfx} pasted in as a resource would have to carry a hand-written generated region \u8212? field declarations, a build method, and the meta lines saying which component sits where \u8212? and every one of those has to match what the parser expects, or the form opens in the Design tab as an empty canvas and the first save quietly deletes it. The wizard builds the form model and lets the writer write it, exactly as pressing {\b Apply to code} does. The file cannot disagree with the parser, because the generator produced it.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj What you get is a project that {\b runs} on the first press: sign in, count, sign out \u8212? with the counter living in the session rather than in a {\f1\fs18 Shared} field, because on a desktop that difference is invisible and in a browser it is the whole game.\par
\pard\sb160\sa160\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\par
\pard\plain\page\keepn\sb0\sa280\qc\f2\b\fs52\cf2 Part VII \u8212? Mobile\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The same language, the same compiler and a designer of the same shape now reach the phone and the tablet as well. A mobile form is a {\f1\fs18 .rfx} file like any other; what differs is the banner at its head ({\f1\fs18 ' @tsbmob}), the API it binds against ({\f1\fs18 tsb.mobile}) and the three backends that draw it \u8212? {\f1\fs18 android.widget} with ConstraintLayout, UIKit through MobiVM for iOS, and, for the preview and for a desktop run, Swing through the ported {\f1\fs18 java.desktop}.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Until 19.09.2026 this lived in a plugin of its own for Android Studio. It does not any more: {\b there is one plugin, in IntelliJ IDEA, and mobile sits in it beside desktop and web.} One {\f1\fs18 New RapidFX Project\u8230?}, one {\i Run} button, one editor that knows {\f1\fs18 .rfx}.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb400\sa140\f2\b\fs34\cf2 24 The mobile designer\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b tsbDesignerMobile} is the mobile form designer. It is the same program as the desktop designer in all but its catalogue and its backends: a palette on the left, the form in the middle, everything about the selected component on the right, and what it writes is a plain {\f1\fs18 .rfx} file that reads back byte for byte.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 A window of its own\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The designer opens in a window of its own, not as an editor tab \u8212? and that is not a matter of taste. {\f1\fs18 UIManager} applies to the whole JVM, so a canvas embedded in the IDE cannot install a look-and-feel without repainting the IDE around itself; it would inevitably paint in whatever the IDE wears. With {\f1\fs18 renderer = swing} it is FlatLaf that draws on the device, through the same {\f1\fs18 java.desktop} port \u8212? so a canvas in the IDE's appearance would show not a different picture but a wrong one. In its own JVM the designer installs FlatLaf properly and the canvas is the same drawing the device gets.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The designer is carried inside the plugin as a resource jar and unpacked into the IDE's system directory on first use, keyed by the content's own SHA-256. If a designer ever behaves like an older version, look in the IDE cache directory before looking at the source.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The device question comes first\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A new form asks two things \u8212? name and device \u8212? and the device is asked first, because afterwards it can only be answered by redrawing every form. Everything else \u8212? the name, the package \u8212? is one line in {\f1\fs18 rfxmobile.properties} and changeable at any time.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Phone, tablet and large-phone geometries differ in size and in their safe areas; the designer's canvas matches the project's device and orientation, so the preview is the same shape the device is. Choose {\b portrait} or {\b landscape} at creation, and, if you want, that the app is locked to the one orientation.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 One catalogue, three canvases\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj On the form are a label and a button to begin with \u8212? two widgets rather than none, so that both halves of the file show at once: a property set below the banner and an empty handler body above it. What the catalogue offers is drawn identically on all three backends; where a backend cannot do a thing, the designer does not offer it. Colours are one place this was earned rather than assumed: a component's {\f1\fs18 background} and its foreground {\f1\fs18 color} are honoured on Android, on iOS and in the Swing preview alike, verified on the device rather than by eye.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb400\sa140\f2\b\fs34\cf2 25 Building and running on a device\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The Run button, and the target beside it\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b Run} sits in the toolbar beside the button the IDE starts its own projects with. One click builds the project and runs it; the little arrow next to it switches between {\b desktop}, {\b Android} and {\b iOS} and remembers the choice per project \u8212? because whoever works on a layout all afternoon runs on the desktop, where a round takes a second instead of a simulator start.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj What is built is the {\b directory}, not the file: a mobile project is a folder of {\f1\fs18 .rfx} files, and which of them starts is the project's answer ({\f1\fs18 rfxmobile.properties}), not the editor's. The build runs line by line into a console with a progress indicator and a cancel button. The work is done by {\f1\fs18 rfxmobile}, the mobile toolchain, which the plugin carries beside its own jar.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The starter project runs on the first press \u8212? type a name, forward, back \u8212? in about {\b 2 s on the desktop, 3 s on Android and 19 s on iOS}, all three out of the same folder.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Android, without Android Studio\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Android needs the Android SDK, but not Android Studio. {\b Tools \u9656? Set Up Android SDK\u8230?} installs it, with the licences shown one by one and an emulator device; the SDK is found through {\f1\fs18 ANDROID_HOME}, {\f1\fs18 ANDROID_SDK_ROOT} or the default folder on each OS. {\b Run on Android} offers to start the emulator when nothing is connected.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Two rules for the emulator, each of which costs an afternoon if unknown:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Exactly one Android device may be running} \u8212? not two emulators, and not an emulator beside a\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj phone on a cable. The install step does not name a device to {\f1\fs18 adb}, so with two it cannot know which is meant and the build waits for an unambiguity that will not come.\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b Whoever switches the emulator off with the power button must close its window too.} The power\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj button switches the device off, not the emulator; the window keeps the AVD occupied, and the next build waits for a device that will not come up.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The desktop simulation has neither problem: it is an ordinary window with a close button, and several side by side do not disturb each other.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 iOS is built on a Mac\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The iOS build calls {\f1\fs18 xcrun}, {\f1\fs18 codesign} and the simulator, and Apple's SDK licence permits Apple hardware only \u8212? so iOS builds on a Mac and nowhere else, and says so in one sentence at the start rather than after a long download. MobiVM's ahead-of-time compiler is 127 MB and is fetched on the first iOS build into {\f1\fs18 ~/.rfxmobile/}, once per machine, against a published checksum; it does not ship in the plugin. Android, the desktop and the web build on any machine as they are.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 A signed APK or IPA\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Below the run actions, kept apart because it is a different intent, sit {\b Build a signed APK} and {\b Build a signed IPA}: a release build takes a signing key and produces a file somebody uploads. The password is asked for in a dialog, not on a console \u8212? under an IDE there is no console to ask through. Where the password comes from, and where it must never be kept, is its own note: a signing key belongs in an environment variable or a keychain, never in the project.\par
\pard\sb160\sa160\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\par
\pard\plain\page\keepn\sb0\sa280\qc\f2\b\fs52\cf2 Part VIII \u8212? Packaging\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A program that compiles and runs is not yet a program someone can install. The last step turns a desktop or web application into a native installer \u8212? an {\f1\fs18 .exe}, an {\f1\fs18 .msi}, a {\f1\fs18 .dmg}, a {\f1\fs18 .pkg} or an AppImage \u8212? with a Java runtime inside it, so that whoever receives it needs nothing installed first. That step is {\b tsbDeploy}, and since it is carried in the same plugin, it is one menu away from the program you just built.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb400\sa140\f2\b\fs34\cf2 26 Packaging with tsbDeploy\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 What it produces\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj tsbDeploy builds native installers for {\b Windows, macOS and Linux}, each for {\b x86_64 and aarch64}, and it builds them {\b from any one of those hosts} \u8212? no CI, no {\f1\fs18 jpackage}, no npm.\par
