\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The {\f1\fs18 .pkg} is the one exception to {\i from any host}: it is produced on a macOS build host only, because it uses Apple's {\f1\fs18 pkgbuild}.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 The runtime comes from the target, not the host\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Every installer carries its own Java runtime, built with {\f1\fs18 jlink} for the platform it targets rather than the one you are on. tsbDeploy keeps a local JDK repository ({\f1\fs18 ~/.tsbdeploy/jdks}) of Liberica Standard JDK 25 for all six target platforms; {\f1\fs18 jlink} and {\f1\fs18 jdeps} run on your machine, the {\f1\fs18 jmods} come from the target, and only the modules your program actually uses go in. So a Windows aarch64 installer built on an Intel Mac carries a correct, minimal aarch64 Windows runtime.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Two kinds of project\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj tsbDeploy takes the program in one of two shapes, and the choice is a switch:\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b A JAR list} \u8212? the output of a Maven or Gradle build; or\par
\pard\plain\fi-260\li520\sb0\sa90\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1 \u8226?\tab {\b A finished application folder} with no build system at all: the main jar, a {\f1\fs18 lib/} folder and a\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj resources folder. That layout lands unchanged in the installation root \u8212? which is the application's working directory \u8212? so a manifest {\f1\fs18 Class-Path} and relative paths like {\f1\fs18 resources/logo.png} keep working. {\f1\fs18 jdeps} considers every jar in {\f1\fs18 lib/}, and the main class may come from the manifest.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj A RapidFX build is the second shape exactly: {\b Build \u9656? Build RapidFX Jar} writes {\f1\fs18 build/<Name>.jar} with {\f1\fs18 lib/} beside it, the {\f1\fs18 LICENSE}/{\f1\fs18 NOTICE} of the project, and \u8212? for a web program \u8212? {\f1\fs18 tsbweb.properties}. tsbDeploy takes that folder and turns it into installers.\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 From the IDE\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The plugin can take the application straight from a run configuration \u8212? build the module, pack the classes and libraries, read the main class \u8212? and it brings its own {\b tsbDeploy} run configuration for the toolbar. A RapidFX project offers {\b Package with tsbDeploy\u8230?} directly. Behind the menu is the same tool the standalone Swing application and the command line use; the IDE only contributes the two things it is good at, a project to read and a console to report into.\par
\pard\plain\li360\ri200\sb140\sa180\sl276\slmult1\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw40\brdrcf5\brsp120\f0\fs20\cf1 tsbDeploy also ships as a {\b standalone plugin} and as a {\b Swing desktop application}, for packaging programs that were not written in RapidFX. The standalone plugin and the combined RapidFX plugin are marked incompatible with each other, so only one is ever installed: whoever has RapidFX has tsbDeploy already.\par
\pard\sb0\sa0\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 A native launcher, and honest installers\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj The program is started by a small native launcher written in Rust \u8212? no console window on Windows, and a menu entry created on an AppImage's first run. An optional splash screen (PNG, GIF or JPEG) covers the moment before the first window.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Every installer carries a {\f1\fs18 LICENSES.md} with the notices for the launcher, the setup program, the AppImage runtime and the Java runtime, each naming where its source can be had; the source archives of the copyleft components can be placed in the output folder as well. The launcher and the setup program are themselves under {\b MIT OR Apache-2.0}, so the installers they make carry only the usual attribution, not a copyleft obligation. The corresponding sources of OpenJDK and libfuse live permanently at [thorstenstueker/tsbdeploy-sources](https://github.com/thorstenstueker/tsbdeploy-sources).\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb280\sa100\f2\b\fs26\cf1 Signing credentials live in the environment, never in a file\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Signing reads its credentials only from environment variables \u8212? {\f1\fs18 CERTIFICATE_CHAIN}, {\f1\fs18 PRIVATE_KEY}, {\f1\fs18 PRIVATE_KEY_PASSWORD} \u8212? and never from a file committed beside the project. That is the same rule the mobile signing follows, and for the same reason: a key in the repository is a key everyone who clones it has.\par
\pard\sb160\sa160\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw8\brdrcf4\par
\pard\plain\keepn\sb400\sa140\f2\b\fs34\cf2 Closing note\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj Four things in this manual are worth carrying away more than the rest.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b The JDK is not a foreign country.} {\f1\fs18 Files.readString}, a {\f1\fs18 JTable}, a JDBC {\f1\fs18 PreparedStatement} \u8212? none of them needs a wrapper, an adapter or a translation table. Read the Javadoc, apply the fifteen rules of Chapter 11, and write the line.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b One rule decides whether a program survives its second user.} {\f1\fs18 Shared} is for what everybody may see alike; {\f1\fs18 SessionStatic} is for what belongs to one person. The cost of choosing the safer one is seven nanoseconds.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b One form runs in four places.} Desktop, web, Android and iOS out of the same {\f1\fs18 .rfx} file \u8212? not a port and not a subset, but the same components drawn by a different backend. Swing computes the geometry without a screen, and a look and feel can be asked to paint into SVG or onto a phone instead of into desktop pixels.\par
\pard\plain\sb0\sa140\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21\cf1\qj {\b The last step is not an afterthought.} A program nobody can install is not finished. tsbDeploy makes the installer \u8212? for six platforms, from the one you are on, with the runtime inside \u8212? so that "it runs here" becomes "it runs for them".\par
}
